<h2 id="siri-ai">Siri AI</h2>
<p id="p75">It’s been challenging for me to think and write about Siri AI this summer, and <a href="https://www.macstories.net/stories/siri-ai-and-apples-trickle-up-strategy/" rel="noopener noreferrer">I’ve already explained why</a>: as a person whose workflow now revolves around agentic tools, I was not impressed by Siri AI at first.</p>
<p id="p76">I don’t think I was the only “power user” on Apple platforms who’s had this thought since getting their hands on the first beta of iOS 27 in June. If you’re reading this, chances are you’re pretty well versed in what models by OpenAI, Anthropic, or others now offer in terms of assistant features, web-based app integrations, memory, long-running tasks, and more. The product surface of Siri AI – the tentpole feature of iOS 27 that Apple is <em>finally</em> shipping after a two-year delay – has very little in common with those other agents or assistants, whatever you want to call them. Technically, yes – they are all based on a large language model under the hood. From <a href="https://www.reddit.com/r/Applelntelligence/comments/1uz0ezk/new_apple_intelligence_cloud_model_only_scores_62/" rel="noopener noreferrer">a benchmark perspective</a>, Apple’s new Foundation models are vastly behind the latest state of the AI frontier currently occupied by four American labs.</p>
<p id="p77">But as a product for people, Siri AI has a threefold advantage that no other company can match:</p>
<ol id="ol78"><li>It’s built right into your phone or computer</li>
<li>It has a built-in knowledge graph about you in the form of personal context</li>
<li>It can act upon, and retrieve data from, the native apps you use every day, from Apple to third-party ones</li>
</ol><p id="p79">And these advantages sum up why I struggled to understand Siri AI at first. Is Siri AI only passable because it has an unfair advantage due to proprietary integrations that other AI models can’t use? Can it stand on its own as a good implementation of AI for most people? Does it fundamentally reinvent how we use our devices, or is it just a lot of hype for a slightly better Siri?</p>
<p id="p80">Unpacking the answers to these questions will be my goal with this chapter. But I’m going to do something unusual and share my takeaways up front, since they frame how I’ve been using and thinking about Siri AI.</p>
<aside class="pull-quote aligncenter" id="aside81"><blockquote>
<p>Siri AI is a breath of fresh air in the AI landscape right now.</p>
</blockquote>
</aside><p id="p82">I find Siri AI’s underlying models to be subpar compared to the competition: for anything beyond a simple knowledge query, I can’t see Siri AI replacing ChatGPT and its superior reasoning modes combined with web search. For simpler queries – the kind that a Google AI overview could also capably answer – the balance of speed and terse formatting is optimal in Siri AI. If I’m watching a movie and want to know how old an actor is, or if I want to know the meaning of a word, Siri AI is perfect for it and I no longer need to reach for ChatGPT or <a href="https://www.macstories.net/reviews/open-minis-is-the-ios-agent-i-wish-siri-ai-could-be/" rel="noopener noreferrer">my personal agent in Open Minis</a>. As we’ll see later, I find the Siri app to be extremely barebones and representative of the state of the art for chatbot design…two years ago.</p>
<p id="p83">The lack of an “agent mode” for Siri AI stings, and it’s why I can’t replace my beloved Codex or <a href="https://hermes-agent.nousresearch.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Hermes Agent</a> setups with it.</p>
<p id="p84">However, Siri AI eventually clicked for me for the opposite reason: it can do things in and <em>with</em> apps that no other third-party agent can. Yes, they are simpler actions, and, no, results aren’t always perfect; but if you love the Apple app ecosystem and prefer using excellent <strong>native</strong> apps to cross-platform web ones, Siri AI is a breath of fresh air in the AI landscape right now.</p>
<p id="p85">See, Siri AI is complicated, polarizing, and almost impossible to agree upon.</p>
<p id="p86">Welcome to AI, Apple.</p>
<h3 id="foundation-models-and-siri-ais-architecture">Foundation Models and Siri AI’s Architecture</h3>
<p id="p87">Before I cover what the new Siri actually looks like and how it is split across a system-wide assistant and a brand new app, let’s talk about what it is and what powers it under the hood.</p>
<p id="p88">The new Siri is powered by a <strong>system orchestrator</strong> that coordinates user requests across different modalities (text, voice, and images) and five new Apple Foundation models, which are:</p>
<ul id="ul89"><li>AFM 3 Core</li>
<li>AFM 3 Core Advanced</li>
<li>ADM 3 Cloud</li>
<li>AFM 3 Cloud</li>
<li>AFM 3 Cloud Pro</li>
</ul><p id="p90">The first two are on-device models, with Core Advanced being exclusive to more recent devices with higher RAM. ADM Cloud is an image diffusion model that runs in the cloud, and which I will not cover in this review since I have no interest in generative images. AFM Cloud and Cloud Pro are cloud models running on Private Cloud Compute, with some differences: while Cloud is optimized for latency and efficiency (both for users, since it’s faster, and for Apple, since it costs less to serve), Cloud Pro is capable of deeper reasoning and agentic tool use, and it does not currently run on Apple-made servers. Instead, Apple teamed up with Google and NVIDIA to host Cloud Pro on NVIDIA GPUs running on Google Cloud, while maintaining the same privacy and security guarantees as “regular” PCC. (Only Apple can deploy software to these nodes, and only Apple can sign that software. More details <a href="https://security.apple.com/blog/expanding-pcc/" rel="noopener noreferrer">here</a>.)</p>
<p id="p91">All of these models were, according to Apple, trained with proprietary data and reinforcement learning, then “refined” with data from frontier Gemini models. Essentially, the new AFM family of models was refined with <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Knowledge_distillation" rel="noopener noreferrer">distilled</a> Gemini outputs. I can’t stress this enough: although Siri AI and AFM may exhibit some behaviors reminiscent of Gemini (as I’ll explain later), Siri AI is not “powered by Gemini” and <strong>there is no Gemini to be found in Apple’s operating systems</strong>. No Gemini model or agentic harness is being used by Apple.</p>
<p id="p92">There are a couple more details worth exploring about the underlying nature of Siri AI. The first concerns what is, in my opinion, the most interesting model of the bunch – the new AFM 3 Core Advanced that runs on-device. For starters, Apple now has a larger local model that is multimodal: Core Advanced supports vision capabilities and powers the advanced dictation and expressive voices of the new Siri. Because of the increased footprint and technical requirements of this model, however, Apple had to restrict it to more modern devices:</p>
<ul id="ul93"><li>iPhone Air, iPhone 17 Pro, iPhone 17 Pro Max, iPad (M4) or later with at least 12 GB of RAM</li>
<li>Mac (M3) or later with at least 12 GB of RAM</li>
<li>Apple Vision Pro with M5 (I always forget this exists)</li>
</ul><p id="p94">The architecture of the model itself is also interesting. As I previously <a href="https://www.macstories.net/linked/the-third-generation-of-apples-foundation-models-and-afm-core-advanced/" rel="noopener noreferrer">explained</a>, while AFM 3 Core is still a 3B model with a dense architecture, AFM 3 Core Advanced is a 20B model that can run on devices with 12 GB of RAM. That’s made possible by a technology that Apple invented for <a href="https://machinelearning.apple.com/research/pruning-large-language" rel="noopener noreferrer">sparse architecture models</a> in which the model is stored in flash memory, activated parameters are locked in per prompt, and only 1 to 4 billion parameters are activated concurrently depending on the user’s request. From Apple:</p>
<blockquote id="blockquote95"><p>
  One area of deep innovation is our most powerful on-device model, AFM 3 Core Advanced. Traditional large language models—whether dense or sparsely activated—require all weights to reside in active memory (DRAM), creating a massive footprint that limits scalability on consumer hardware. To break this barrier, AFM 3 Core Advanced introduces a novel sparsely activated architecture built on Instruction-Following Pruning (IFP), a technique developed by Apple researchers.<br>
  Instead of forcing the entire model into DRAM, the full model is stored in flash memory (NAND). Because NAND-to-DRAM bandwidth is too slow to swap weights token by token, as standard MoE models require, AFM 3 Core Advanced makes routing decisions per prompt. A lightweight, dense block selects a fixed set of experts during initial processing, periodically reselecting them during generation. To minimize data movement, the model relies on a high percentage of always-active “shared experts” alongside input-dependent “routed experts” swapped into DRAM only when needed.
</p></blockquote>
<p id="p96">You don’t need to be an AI expert to understand that what Apple wants to achieve here seems remarkably complex and optimistic. Here we have a company that is two years late to its original promises for an AI-powered Siri, and which is saying it has built a system to orchestrate five models, across multiple modalities, with support for proprietary World Knowledge (since you can now ask Siri general questions about anything, too), and which is going to draw from your personal context on-device as well as integrate with all kinds of third-party apps. And while all of this is happening, the system should know how to split inference across local and cloud models, how to allocate reasoning effort levels, how to coordinate actions across apps, and how to do it all in less than 15 seconds with a nice, minimalistic UI.</p>
<p id="p97">When I started working on this review, I struggled to visualize and understand the new Siri AI architecture in my head, so I built something with <a href="https://www.anthropic.com/claude-fable-and-mythos-5-1" rel="noopener noreferrer">Fable 5.1</a> and <a href="https://openai.com/index/gpt-6-astra/" rel="noopener noreferrer">GPT-6 Astra</a> to help me, and I’m embedding it in this review because maybe it’ll help you too. For the following visualization, I started from Apple’s official Siri AI graphic that the company <a href="https://www.apple.com/newsroom/2026/06/apple-introduces-siri-ai-a-profoundly-more-capable-and-personal-assistant/" rel="noopener noreferrer">shared</a> at WWDC, then I combined it with the Apple Intelligence Report that can be exported directly from the Settings app in iOS and iPadOS 27. The report contains all Siri AI requests that were issued by the user over the span of 7 or 30 days, and it includes details on the steps taken by Siri AI, what tools were loaded, how many tokens were in the system prompt, what third-party apps returned via App Intents, how much time each step took, and more.</p>
<p id="p98">The interactive visualization shows you some examples from my real requests – typos and everything – that were performed with Apple’s built-in apps as well as <a href="https://askplay.app/" rel="noopener noreferrer">AskPlay</a> and <a href="https://emojicountdown.app/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Emoji Countdown</a>. As you can see, it’s a very intricate system that is executed remarkably fast by Siri AI and the system orchestrator.</p>
<p id="p99"><a href="https://www.macstories.net/stories/ios-and-ipados-27-review/" rel="noopener noreferrer">View this on MacStories.</a></p>
<hr id="hr100"><p id="p101">By and large, what Apple has built works, and as a user of Apple devices and the new Siri AI, <strong>you will never see any of its underlying complexity.</strong></p>
<p id="p102">The new Siri AI is far from perfect and objectively behind the state of frontier AI models, but it’s also doing things that no other company is, and I firmly believe it is going to be good enough for millions of people.</p>
<p id="p103">If you’re coming from any other AI product from OpenAI, Anthropic, Google, or the rest of the industry, you must reset your expectations in more ways than one when it comes to Siri AI.</p>
<p id="p104">First, unless you’ve been on the beta and have already let your device go through its new indexing process, you’ll probably have to wait a few days until you a) get access to Siri AI and b) can use it to its full extent. In order to work properly, Siri AI needs to build an index of your context and apps, and this process isn’t quick. You can speed up the indexing by keeping your phone on Wi-Fi and connected to a charger, but it’s not immediate. You can monitor its progress via a system message displayed in the Settings app; in my experience, while the initial indexing takes a while, subsequent updates to the index are much faster, if not downright immediate in some cases (such as downloading a new app and asking Siri AI questions about it).</p>
<p id="p105">Second, you should let go of a bunch of UI conventions you’ve probably grown accustomed to when using ChatGPT or Claude over the past two years. There are no model pickers, reasoning selectors, skills, @mentions of plugins, context windows, memories, “fast modes”, or usage resets in Siri AI. This new product embodies the quintessential “it just works” Apple mantra – sometimes to a fault. The new Siri looks and works like a more conversational Siri that also comes with a dedicated Siri chatbot app. There is nothing you can configure or tweak about it besides, <em>well</em>, using your devices with different apps.</p>
<p id="p106">There is a fascinating dichotomy occurring between the underlying complexity of Siri AI and the utter simplicity presented to users. Most frontier AI models and products wear their complexity on their sleeves, with different levers to pull and settings to tweak if you want to get the most out of them. Apple isn’t giving users this kind of customization (or responsibility?) yet, and I think it’s the correct choice to ease <a href="https://appleinsider.com/articles/26/01/29/apple-reaches-25-billion-active-devices-after-record-breaking-quarter" rel="noopener noreferrer">over two billion people</a> into the uncomfortable world of AI.</p>
<p id="p107"><!--nextpage--></p>
<h3 id="the-new-siri">The New Siri</h3>
<p id="p108">Let’s talk about the actual product.</p>
<p id="p109">The new Siri presents itself across five different surfaces:</p>
<ul id="ul110"><li>Voice</li>
<li>Text</li>
<li>Siri app</li>
<li>Camera</li>
<li>Contextual menus in apps</li>
</ul><p id="p111">I’ll save the Siri app for later; I want to focus on the system-wide Siri experience for now.</p>
<p id="p112">At its most basic level, you’ll immediately recognize the new Siri by its design. The old glowing animation around the device’s bezels is gone, replaced by a floating dark orb made out of Liquid Glass that, on iPhone, smoothly descends from the Dynamic Island to listen to you. The orb pulses slightly to indicate it’s listening, and as you speak your requests to Siri, its multicolor waveform (the only place where Siri kept its former colors) reacts in real-time. The fluidity and responsiveness of the animations are top notch, which shouldn’t come as a surprise from the team that gave us the Dynamic Island, Liquid Glass’ morphing transitions, and the iPhone Duo’s flavor of iOS.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/10/shortcut-upload-1789048730359-e78cc1dfce.png" alt="Old and new Siri."><p class="image-caption">Old and new Siri.</p></div>
<div class="media-wrapper video-autoplay js-video-autoplay aligncenter" id="div114">
            <video data-autoplay="yes" width="675" height="1380" data-is-retina="yes" preload="auto" playsinline controls><source src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/12/iphone-siri-orb-framed-1789217330629-0b5b7053b1.mp4" type="video/mp4"><source src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/12/iphone-siri-orb-framed-1789217330629-0b5b7053b1.webm" type="video/webm"><source src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/12/iphone-siri-orb-framed-1789217330629-0b5b7053b1.ogv" type="video/ogg"></source></source></source></video><div class="caption">
<p>The new Siri animation.</p>
</div></div>
<p id="p115">The new Siri animation is even more impressive on iPad, where the orb “comes out” of the device’s bezel like a drop of liquid that lands at the top of the screen and, since it’s made of glass, beautifully refracts whatever is underneath. Even better, this fluid transition can be triggered from anywhere in the iPad status bar:</p>
<div class="media-wrapper video-autoplay js-video-autoplay aligncenter aligncenter" id="div116">
            <video data-autoplay="yes" width="1500" height="1150" data-is-retina="yes" preload="auto" playsinline controls><source src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/12/siri-orb-ipad-framed-1789217270098-f5cce35df0.mp4" type="video/mp4"><source src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/12/siri-orb-ipad-framed-1789217270098-f5cce35df0.webm" type="video/webm"><source src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/12/siri-orb-ipad-framed-1789217270098-f5cce35df0.ogv" type="video/ogg"></source></source></source></video><div class="caption">
<p>I can’t stop staring at this.</p>
</div></div>
<p id="p117">As you talk to the new Siri, you’ll see the orb dynamically shrink into a “thinking” activity with a loading indicator, then expand again into a dark, translucent overlay that, unlike other chatbots, presents results visually with a combination of text, images, and custom UIs belonging to the apps whose data is being fetched thanks to Apple’s <a href="https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appintents" rel="noopener noreferrer">App Intents framework</a>. Ask for your tasks, and you’ll see a Reminders snippet; ask for where you went today, and you’ll get a grid of photos; if you want to quickly save an idea, ask Siri to make a note, and you’ll be presented with an inline Notes card at the end. You get the idea.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/10/shortcut-upload-1789049187627-271a1737a7.png" alt="Different examples of the Siri UI."><p class="image-caption">Different examples of the Siri UI.</p></div>
<p id="p119">At any point during a conversation with the new Siri, you can switch to text input by swiping down on the Siri response and start typing. This is part of a larger story in iOS 27: system-wide access to Siri and the unification of Siri and Spotlight. The assistant and the system’s search feature have been merged into the same UI element – a floating search field that can be activated from anywhere by swiping down on the Dynamic Island. Doing this will reveal the new call-to-action for this box: Search or Ask.</p>
<p id="p120">Just as with the previous Spotlight, you’ll see some suggestions for apps you can launch or other activities; the difference is that now the system can determine whether you’re searching for something or formulating a request for Siri. If the latter mode is detected, Spotlight search results will be collapsed, and ‘Ask Siri’ will appear next to your query, indicating that you can hit Return to ask Siri AI a question instead of searching with Spotlight.</p>
<aside class="info-box " id="aside121"><p>If you long-press an app suggestion in Siri for iOS 27, you can choose to ‘Show More Apps’ and jump from four suggested apps to eight. You can revert this choice later.</p>
<p class="icon"><svg viewbox="0 0 120 120" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><circle clip-rule="evenodd" cx="60" cy="60" fill-rule="evenodd" r="60"></circle><path d="m44 89v-5h13.2v-28.6h-13.1v-5h19.1v33.6h12.8v5zm10.8-53.1c0-2.8 2.2-4.9 5-4.9s5 2.1 5 4.9-2.2 5-5 5-5-2.1-5-5z" fill="#fff"></path></svg></p>
</aside><div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/10/shortcut-upload-1789049452806-dcbdb8b87e.png" alt="From the same search box, you can either search with Spotlight or ask Siri."><p class="image-caption">From the same search box, you can either search with Spotlight or ask Siri.</p></div>
<p id="p123">It takes a while to get used to the idea that you can now search and ask questions from anywhere on your iPhone without using your voice, and it’s awesome. iPhone users never had the ability to invoke Spotlight from outside the Home Screen when using apps; in the three months I’ve spent using iOS 27, I can’t tell you how accustomed I’ve become to jumping between apps or searching inside them without leaving the app I’m in.</p>
<p id="p124">When using the new Siri and alternating between search and ask modes, you’ll never be able to tell which local model is being used or if Siri is going off to the cloud and picking one of the two models running there. You can <em>infer</em> what’s going on (if a request is taking a bit longer, the underlying Siri orchestrator probably decided to use extended reasoning in the cloud), and there are ways to inspect your Siri AI reports later, but – by design – all complexity is abstracted from the experience when Siri AI is visible to the user.</p>
<p id="p125">What you will notice, however, is the <strong>new Siri voice</strong>.</p>
<h4 id="the-new-siri-voice">The New Siri Voice</h4>
<p id="p126">The new expressive voices for Siri AI are powered by Apple’s larger on-device model and, as such, are exclusive to more recent devices. They are a substantial jump in terms of voice quality and expressivity compared to the older Siri voice in iOS 26: tone and cadence feel more natural, enunciation sounds less robotic than before, and, most of all, the voices can be customized.</p>
<p id="p127">Once you have the new Siri AI, head over to Settings → Siri → Voice, and you’ll be presented with a gorgeous new voice preview and personalization screen that lets you pick from a selection of unlabeled voice types across six English accents (American, Australian, British, Indian, Irish, South African). You can tap the colored dots to preview each voice, and Siri will speak a longer demo sentence than iOS 26. Amusingly, a part of the sentence is about the fact that otters hold hands so they don’t drift apart. I did not need to know this, and I can’t stop thinking about this fact now.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/10/shortcut-upload-1789049684401-906aab9cb1.png" alt="Siri’s new voice customization screen."><p class="image-caption">Siri’s new voice customization screen.</p></div>
<p id="p129">Anyway, only American and British voices support personalization in the form of pace and expressivity sliders. Pace is easy to understand: drag it all the way to the right to <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z9G1Mf6TZRs" rel="noopener noreferrer">make it go fast</a>. For my taste, I have set the pace to the fourth notch of the slider. Expressivity is a little trickier to understand: it sets the voice’s underlying tone and how the assistant switches intonation between different phrases. Personally, I’ve set mine all the way to the right.</p>
<p id="p130">There is no denying that the new Siri voices are vastly superior to what Siri sounded like before. Here, let’s do a comparison between Siri in iOS 26 and Siri in iOS 27:</p>
<div class="media-wrapper video-autoplay js-video-autoplay aligncenter aligncenter aligncenter" id="div131">
            <video data-autoplay="no" width="960" height="540" data-is-retina="no" preload="auto" playsinline controls><source src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/12/old-vs-new-siri-web-1080p-1789231630086-487e08a20a.mp4" type="video/mp4"><source src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/12/old-vs-new-siri-web-1080p-1789231630086-487e08a20a.webm" type="video/webm"><source src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/12/old-vs-new-siri-web-1080p-1789231630086-487e08a20a.ogv" type="video/ogg"></source></source></source></video><div class="caption">
<p>Make sure to watch until the end for a…surprising response. (Sound on.)</p>
</div></div>
<p id="p132">The new voices are impressive for something that is being generated on-device, and I think most iPhone users upgrading to Siri AI now will notice the difference and think that Siri sounds better than ever. It’s remarkable that all of this is being generated locally across multiple accents.</p>
<p id="p133">In the context of the broader AI industry and voice experiences, however, the new Siri voice still trails the frontier. As far as voice itself is concerned, <a href="https://elevenlabs.io/v3" rel="noopener noreferrer">ElevenLabs’ v3</a> model sounds more natural than Siri AI and was introduced over a year ago in June 2025. I would argue that even <a href="https://huggingface.co/spaces/hexgrad/Kokoro-TTS" rel="noopener noreferrer">Kokoro</a>, an open-weight text-to-speech model with 82 million parameters that supports more voices and the ability to create custom ones, sounds nicer than Siri. (You can <a href="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/12/audio-1789203278119-8b74dbd10f.wav" rel="noopener noreferrer">listen to this very paragraph generated with Kokoro</a>.) And then there’s GPT-Live, the frontier <a href="https://openai.com/index/continuous-voice-interaction-with-gpt-live/" rel="noopener noreferrer">bidirectional voice model</a> by OpenAI that powers ChatGPT Voice, <a href="https://openai.com/index/introducing-gpt-live/" rel="noopener noreferrer">introduced earlier this year</a>. GPT-Live is, by all measures, the current state of the art for natural assistant/person conversations since it supports interruptions, real-time tone and cadence adjustments (you can ask ChatGPT to slow down or whisper), and long-running back-and-forth conversations that Siri AI is simply not designed for.</p>
<p id="p134">This is an important point: if you think that Siri AI will allow you to ramble to a digital assistant for 10 minutes and have a “conversation” with it like you can with ChatGPT Voice, you’re going to be disappointed. Siri AI is not capable of that kind of voice interaction yet. Siri AI’s voice experience is still based on the classic “ask question/wait/listen to answer” pattern from the older Siri. It’s slightly more conversational, and it sounds better than ever, but it can’t hold a prolonged conversation: you can’t go for a walk and start talking to Siri AI to brainstorm with it like many folks do with ChatGPT Voice.</p>
<p id="p135">To give you even more context, here’s a comparison between Siri AI and ChatGPT Voice, recorded using the GPT-Live-1 model for paid users:</p>
<div class="media-wrapper video-autoplay js-video-autoplay aligncenter aligncenter aligncenter aligncenter" id="div136">
            <video data-autoplay="no" width="960" height="540" data-is-retina="no" preload="auto" playsinline controls><source src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/12/c0093-web-1080p-1789231568760-0a7b8a741c.mp4" type="video/mp4"><source src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/12/c0093-web-1080p-1789231568760-0a7b8a741c.webm" type="video/webm"><source src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/12/c0093-web-1080p-1789231568760-0a7b8a741c.ogv" type="video/ogg"></source></source></source></video><div class="caption">
<p>Siri AI and ChatGPT Voice are two fundamentally different products, and OpenAI’s advantage in voice is obvious…until I switch languages. (Sound on.)</p>
</div></div>
<p id="p137">Comparisons between Siri and other AI-based voice experiences are a good indicator of where things stand right now. Apple is still behind the top American labs when it comes to sheer model quality, and can’t yet enable experiences we’ve seen in other chatbots. Over a billion people can chat for free with <a href="https://openai.com/index/gpt-5-5-instant/" rel="noopener noreferrer">GPT-5.5 Instant</a> and GPT-Live-1-mini in ChatGPT, and both models are superior to Apple’s roster of five Foundation models – not to mention that they are available worldwide and are multilingual by default. Siri AI is only launching in English, despite the two-year delay. The comparison to the rest of the industry isn’t even fair.</p>
<p id="p138">At the same time, not everyone is interested in or trusts the likes of OpenAI and Anthropic for their digital assistant needs. In fact, I’m willing to bet that there are hundreds of millions of people who just wanted a better Siri because, frankly, the old one was an awful product that Apple should have been embarrassed by.</p>
<p id="p139">The new Siri is a much smarter, more capable, and nicer Siri than before. On a scale from “awful” to “state-of-the-art AI frontier”, Siri AI sits comfortably in the middle. It is a solid product that finally gives Apple users a good digital assistant that works well enough for most requests. At the same time, it is a product that will not “wow” AI power users and proves that Apple still has a lot of work to do if they want to compete in this space, especially if their newfound rival (and <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/08/31/apple-openai-lawsuit-trade-secret-theft-evidence/" rel="noopener noreferrer">defendant</a>) enters the hardware market in the near future.</p>
<p id="p140">And since multiple things can be true at the same time (and people can hold multiple opinions – what a concept), it is also undeniable that Siri AI delivers a “good” experience across the more challenging spectrum of an entire operating system, which neither OpenAI nor Anthropic can currently match.</p>
<p id="p141">But <em>how</em> is it better?</p>
<p id="p142"><!--nextpage--></p>
<h3 id="siri-as-a-general-assistant">Siri as a General Assistant</h3>
<p id="p143">Put simply: Siri is smarter overall for most things and has pretty good and up-to-date world knowledge baked in. When I ask something like “what tasks do I have today”, the new Siri gives me both my reminders and calendar events, which the old Siri couldn’t. Last week, I asked about what Liam and Noel Gallagher had been up to recently, and after a few seconds, Siri rightly pointed out they’d just attended the <a href="https://www.labiennale.org/en/cinema/2026/venice-open-out-competition/oasis-don%E2%80%99t-look-back-anger" rel="noopener noreferrer">Venice Film Festival</a> and that rumors of a <a href="https://www.nme.com/news/music/oasis-2027-tour-drone-display-manchester-reveal-announcement-date-and-time-3968153" rel="noopener noreferrer">2027 Oasis tour</a> were circulating. When I ask Siri to send my most recent photo to Silvia, the assistant prompts me to pick a recent photo, then prepares an iMessage preview, and confirms with me that I want to send it. As we’ll see in a later section, working with Siri AI and apps can be a remarkably efficient experience, and – once again – it’s leagues above what the old, dumb Siri used to be.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/10/shortcut-upload-1789050244739-bf834973d5.png" alt="The new Siri can just do things."><p class="image-caption">The new Siri can just do things.</p></div>
<p id="p145">As a result of all this, I find myself defaulting to Siri AI for most “quick questions” about on-device things or web searches that I would have completed with Google AI overviews before. Since Apple’s world knowledge graph is good and up-to-date enough, whenever I have a question that doesn’t need a deep research report, I use neither ChatGPT nor Google anymore: I just ask Siri. If I want to go deeper on a topic, then, yes, I tend to switch back to another agent, but for any simple factual question, Siri AI is my default search engine now.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/shortcut-upload-1789112928286-a71d35de2c.png" alt="Apple’s world knowledge engine supports recent results, and it’s a dramatic improvement for quick web searches compared to the old Siri."><p class="image-caption">Apple’s world knowledge engine supports recent results, and it’s a dramatic improvement for quick web searches compared to the old Siri.</p></div>
<p id="p147">When writing this review, I frequently had to go back and test the old Siri in iOS 26 and, let me tell you: after using Siri AI in iOS 27, I was <em>shocked</em> to re-experience just how <em>bad</em> Siri was. The effect is quite dramatic: the new Siri is no longer plagued by those useless “I found these on the web” results like before, nor does it go off to ChatGPT to get some additional help. In most scenarios, the new Siri just answers and provides you with a good-quality response. If you have multiple Apple devices, I suggest leaving one on iOS/iPadOS 26 for a short while so you can have some fun comparing the two versions of Siri side by side, which will make you appreciate the new one even more.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/shortcut-upload-1789113110626-f028014281.png" alt="Presented without comment."><p class="image-caption">Presented without comment.</p></div>
<aside class="pull-quote aligncenter" id="aside149"><blockquote>
<p>For any simple factual question, Siri AI is my default search engine now.</p>
</blockquote>
</aside><p id="p150">Beyond system-wide access, new voices, and superior world knowledge compared to iOS 26, Apple’s big bet with Siri AI is going past what other self-contained agents and chatbots can do. When you’re using ChatGPT Work or Claude Cowork, or perhaps even more complex products such as Hermes Agent or OpenClaw, you are always recreating your profile, workspace, and preferences in a silo. This is why memory is such a sought-after currency in the AI market: by locking users into their ecosystem, different AI providers can make it less enticing to switch to a competitor. If you’re OpenAI and <a href="https://www.wired.com/story/openai-dev-day-sam-altman-chatgpt-apps/" rel="noopener noreferrer">aiming to build an operating system</a>, it makes a lot of sense to lay a foundation in the form of memories, <a href="https://www.macstories.net/stories/hands-on-with-computer-history-openais-take-on-agent-memory/" rel="noopener noreferrer">computer history</a>, and other personalization tools that make users feel like they can’t switch to anything else.</p>
<p id="p151">Despite the efforts of AI labs over the past year to go beyond chatbots and ship a semblance of a platform, the truth is that only Apple and Google actually have a platform where AI can be deeply embedded and serve as an app-agnostic assistant layer. In simpler terms:</p>
<p id="p152">Apple thinks you’re going to be using Siri AI because it works with any app on your phone, gathers context about you automatically from any app, and can also see what’s on your screen no matter the app you’re using.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/13/shortcut-upload-1789284775082-93dc449ed3.png" alt="Siri AI can regularly ask you to confirm which app you want to use as default for any particular domain (left). In these examples, I asked for some tasks in Stuff and logged an espresso in Alyx."><p class="image-caption">Siri AI can regularly ask you to confirm which app you want to use as default for any particular domain (left). In these examples, I asked for some tasks in Stuff and logged an espresso in Alyx.</p></div>
<p id="p154">These capabilities are what Apple refers to as on-screen awareness and personal context, and although I’ve experienced some issues with them, I’m also very excited about their potential: they’re the features that have made Siri AI stand out the most for me.</p>
<h4 id="on-screen-awareness-and-personal-context">On-Screen Awareness and Personal Context</h4>
<p id="p155">The idea of on-screen awareness is straightforward enough: Siri can now see what’s on screen and act on it. It may not sound like a big deal, but that would be a wrong assumption: the system Apple has built around on-screen awareness is a great example of how we can weave AI features throughout the interactions we have every day.</p>
<p id="p156">At a high level, whenever you’re looking at something on-screen and want to ask Siri a question about it, you can invoke the assistant and just ask. Being able to ask Siri questions about the current state of an app visible on screen isn’t new – in fact, <a href="https://www.macstories.net/stories/ios-9-review/7/" rel="noopener noreferrer">it was introduced in iOS 9</a> with the ability to say “Remind me about this”. What’s different now is that we have the power of an LLM to process not just a deeplink to an app’s screen, but an entire set of annotated data points (provided that app developers support them) that give Siri plenty of information about what’s visible on the user’s device.</p>
<p id="p157">Under the hood, iOS 27 apps can embed invisible annotations about any content shown on screen, so that Siri AI gets direct metadata from each compatible app instead of merely taking a screenshot and performing OCR on it. For instance, if you’re looking at a page that mentions an event, you can say “add this to my calendar”, and Siri will know what you mean and create an event with the title and dates extracted from the visible page. But it goes deeper than that. Say that you’re looking at an album in the Music app, and you want to play a specific song but can’t tap the screen. Say “play the third one” and Siri will know that you mean the third one in the list and start playback immediately because it’s not screenshotting what’s on screen and then searching for the song: the song is an entity that’s been annotated with an identifier that tells Siri exactly what to play.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/10/shortcut-upload-1789050493510-dfc6845d4d.png" alt="Adding a concert date to my calendar with Siri."><p class="image-caption">Adding a concert date to my calendar with Siri.</p></div>
<p id="p159">Once you understand the flexibility of this on-screen system in Apple’s own apps (and, hopefully, lots of third-party ones soon, too), you can start mixing and matching requests, which is where the fun begins. I was in the Music app, and I wanted to know more about a specific song of an album, so I asked: <em>“Did the first one also come out as a single, not as part of this album?”</em> Sure enough, Siri AI saw that I was looking at Oasis’ <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Masterplan_(album)" rel="noopener noreferrer">The Masterplan</a>, that the first song was <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Acquiesce" rel="noopener noreferrer">Acquiesce</a>, and that it was, in fact, originally released as a B-side to the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Some_Might_Say" rel="noopener noreferrer">Some Might Say</a> single.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/shortcut-upload-1789113262570-8cc9eadb35.png" alt="Here, I asked for more information about “the first one” and “the second one”. Siri AI knew what I meant."><p class="image-caption">Here, I asked for more information about “the first one” and “the second one”. Siri AI knew what I meant.</p></div>
<p id="p161">Combining on-screen awareness with additional search queries or actions is one of my favorite aspects of Siri AI, and I think Apple’s engineers have done an outstanding job integrating this in their apps and creating APIs for developers to do the same. When looking at a text in the Messages app, you can say “love that last message” and Siri AI will add a heart Tapback to it; in Files, you can say stuff like “send the third document to John” and Siri will know from your context (more below) and on-screen awareness that you mean a specific person and the third item in a list.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/shortcut-upload-1789113439060-44bfa34250.png" alt="“Send the second file to John” is what I asked here."><p class="image-caption">“Send the second file to John” is what I asked here.</p></div>
<p id="p163">How effective on-screen awareness turns out to be will depend on how well third-party developers support it. From what I have seen in Apple’s apps so far, though, I believe pervasive, reliable on-screen awareness has the potential to reshape how we think of Siri. It takes a while to accept that Siri can be this good and versatile: I still struggle sometimes to remember that, when looking at Reminders, I can say “mark the second one as done”, or that I can look at a calendar event and say “email the participants that I’ll be 10 minutes late”, and Siri will draft a message with Mail about it. I think others will go through a similar “reset phase” as they figure out that Siri is actually pretty great for this stuff now.</p>
<aside class="pull-quote aligncenter" id="aside164"><blockquote>
<p>It takes a while to accept that Siri can be this good and versatile.</p>
</blockquote>
</aside><p id="p165">Personal context is the trickier Siri characteristic to explain, as well as the area where Siri AI has struggled the most for me over the past three months.</p>
<p id="p166">The theory is the following: by building an index of your apps and data, Siri AI is able to draw from your personal context and answer questions without you having to mention an app by name, specify a time period, or be too precise about your question. This is not meant to be proactive assistance (which Siri AI cannot do and is currently the holy grail for AI labs alongside <a href="https://openai.com/index/an-alien-mind/" rel="noopener noreferrer">recursive self-improvement</a>); personal context is more like an automatic “enrichment” of answers that doesn’t require you to ask.</p>
<p id="p167">A popular example used by both Apple and Google is the flight one: ask about when you’re supposed to pick up a friend at the airport, and Siri will tell you that Myke’s flight lands at 3 PM because it has that context from iMessage. In my tests, I’ve seen personal context in action in a handful of ways. One time, I asked where my dogs went the day before, and Siri told me that Ginger was at the beach, which it knew because it had a Ginger album from Photos and indexed photos of her taken at the beach. My query was generic, but the answer was precise. Likewise, when I asked <em>“Did John tell me things I have to do this week?”</em>, Siri gave me a list of things John previously told me over email and iMessage.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/shortcut-upload-1789113615574-8296c495ae.png" alt="Examples of personal context in action. You can always expand Siri snippets to go full-screen and continue typing, chatbot-style."><p class="image-caption">Examples of personal context in action. You can always expand Siri snippets to go full-screen and continue typing, chatbot-style.</p></div>
<p id="p169">You’re not supposed to know that personal context is working, and that can be both a blessing and a curse. When Siri AI does bring up a useful piece of information about you, unprompted, it feels <em>amazing</em>, and it perfectly encapsulates Apple’s competitive advantage in AI: unlike other companies’ assistants, Siri AI can know everything about you as long as you keep using Apple devices.</p>
<p id="p170">On the other hand, I’ve frequently run into scenarios where a question I knew had data to back it up in iMessage couldn’t be answered by Siri AI at all, or where the context about me was simply stale. This, I believe, is a byproduct of Apple’s on-device indexing approach: results don’t get indexed immediately, and the entire thing can feel like a mysterious black box.</p>
<p id="p171">As a result, my mind hasn’t been blown by personal context in Siri AI yet, but I remain hopeful that Apple is on the right track from a strategic perspective. I wonder if more capable hardware and future model updates will be able to help in this regard, letting the index be refreshed more frequently and proactively suggesting more details in new Siri chats.</p>
<p id="p172"><!--nextpage--></p>
<h3 id="the-siri-app">The Siri App</h3>
<p id="p173">The new Siri is no longer just a voice assistant on iOS and iPadOS: it’s also a full-blown app with an icon on your Home Screen. The app holds all of your conversations with Siri and allows you to keep expanding a conversation after your original request, meaning two things: Siri is now also a traditional chatbot, and your Siri sessions are no longer ephemeral since they can be reviewed and continued later.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/shortcut-upload-1789113729420-0bff927ff0.png" alt="The new Siri app on iPad."><p class="image-caption">The new Siri app on iPad.</p></div>
<p id="p175">The problem is that the Siri app is, quite frankly, a pretty bad app. It is extremely barebones compared to the rest of the AI industry and their chatbot offerings, lacking all kinds of integrations and features that people have gotten used to having in ChatGPT or Gemini. It feels like using GPT-4 in ChatGPT from a couple of years ago. Furthermore, it performs badly, with slow animations, visual previews that are frequently missing on screen, and other technical issues that do not meet Apple’s standards of excellent iOS apps (such as Notes, Safari, Reminders, or Journal).</p>
<p id="p176">Really, there isn’t much to say about the Siri app other than it’s nice to have a place where you can revisit and resume previous conversations. By default, the app opens with a “cascading” grid layout that looks great in screenshots but is the most uncomfortable layout I’ve ever used in practice if you want to find a specific chat. Thankfully, you can revert to a classic list mode by tapping a button in the top-right corner of the app.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/shortcut-upload-1789113810755-5c5ffb4b9c.png" alt="You can switch between different view modes, and also tap on citations to see web sources."><p class="image-caption">You can switch between different view modes, and also tap on citations to see web sources.</p></div>
<p id="p178">You can then rename, pin, or delete conversations, and you can tweak three settings for the Siri app in Settings → Siri:</p>
<ul id="ul179"><li>You can choose to open the app to recent conversations or a new conversation</li>
<li>You can keep conversations forever or have them automatically deleted after 30 days or 1 year (there is no custom option)</li>
<li>You can set the preview text for the main view from ‘None’ to five lines of text</li>
</ul><p id="p180">Inside conversations, you can long-press a response to share it as a PDF, copy it, or have Siri’s voice read it aloud (with a nice mini audio player inside the app); you can attach files and photos; and you can press a microphone button to alternate between text and voice input.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/shortcut-upload-1789113976908-379dd64d37.png" alt=""><p class="image-caption"></p></div>
<p id="p182">And…that’s basically it. Coming from ChatGPT, Gemini, Claude, Grok, or any of the Chinese LLMs such as <a href="https://www.kimi.ai/blog/kimi-k3" rel="noopener noreferrer">Kimi</a> and <a href="https://z.ai/blog/glm-5.3" rel="noopener noreferrer">GLM</a>, the Siri app feels like an overly simplistic product that was almost built out of spite, even though Apple had more than two years to put this together. You cannot organize chats in folders or tags. There is no support for reusable commands and instructions in the form of skills. Given Apple’s invisible approach to context, there is no support for any kind of personalization or user-reviewable memory whatsoever. Want to tweak Siri’s writing style? You can’t. Want to review what Siri knows about you? That’s not possible.</p>
<aside class="aside aligncenter aside-wide" id="aside183"><h2 id="chatgpt-extension-in-the-siri-app">ChatGPT Extension in the Siri App</h2>
<p>Wondering what happened to the ChatGPT extension for Apple Intelligence that launched <a href="https://www.macstories.net/stories/apple-intelligence-and-chatgpt-in-18-2/" rel="noopener noreferrer noopener noreferrer">nearly two years ago</a>? It’s still here, and Apple <em>really</em> wants you to ignore it.</p>
<p>In what has to be one of the best-hidden features of iOS 27, you can still summon ChatGPT in Siri’s floating search box or the Siri app by manually tapping into the search field, selecting ‘Ask’, and picking ‘ChatGPT’ as a provider. It’s no surprise given the current litigation between Apple and OpenAI that this integration has been visually demoted so much, and one has to wonder if it’ll be unceremoniously removed as soon as whatever contract is holding it in place expires.</p>
<p>The extension wasn’t great in the first place, so I do not particularly care about it. It’s still not even clear what OpenAI model gets used behind the scenes when you activate this extension; if you want to get an answer from ChatGPT, I recommend just going to the ChatGPT website instead of using the Apple integration. Interestingly, if you ask a question to ChatGPT that involves something Siri AI is capable of doing, the underlying orchestrator will route your request back to Siri automatically.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/shortcut-upload-1789114302517-05b86ede34.png" alt="The ChatGPT extension is still here, and quite hidden. It’s basically useless at this point."><p class="image-caption">The ChatGPT extension is still here, and quite hidden. It’s basically useless at this point.</p></div>
<p>I won’t be sad when this extension is eventually removed and forgotten forever.</p>
</aside><p id="p184">I may be used to working with frontier AI models more than the average user, but it’s hard to deny that this app feels very primitive by any modern standard. Even more annoyingly, Siri isn’t aware of itself at all: Siri AI cannot search and find its previous conversations with you, so while you can resume a conversation later, start a new one and ask <em>“hey, did we ever talk about this topic?”</em>, and Siri AI has no idea what you’re talking about.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/13/shortcut-upload-1789286407635-a673c82f84.png" alt="Unlike an agentic assistant such as Open Minis (right), Siri AI has no idea about its previous context or session history."><p class="image-caption">Unlike an agentic assistant such as Open Minis (right), Siri AI has no idea about its previous context or session history.</p></div>
<p id="p186">This all goes back to a longstanding issue with Apple’s product philosophy: the “it just works” approach can be amazing when everything goes as expected, but it can be detrimental to the user experience when something breaks, or when the user wants to have slightly more control over a particular app. Historically, in my iOS and iPadOS reviews, I’ve pointed out many such examples, from last year’s barebones launch version of <a href="https://www.macstories.net/stories/ios-and-ipados-26-the-macstories-review/12/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Local Capture</a> (which was later improved), to the Home app’s <a href="https://www.macstories.net/stories/ios-and-ipados-13-the-macstories-review/26/#home" rel="noopener noreferrer">automatic grouping in iOS 13</a> and the <a href="https://www.macstories.net/stories/ios-and-ipados-13-the-macstories-review/22/#the-complexities-of-multiwindow" rel="noopener noreferrer">lack of app labels in multiwindowing for iPadOS 13</a>.</p>
<p id="p187">I think the Siri app, and Siri AI in general, suffer from this very issue: in an era where users demand to be able to inspect and tweak what AI knows about them and how it operates for them, Siri AI and its app expose no such controls. I mean, it is literally a product where you can ask questions about apps on your device…and there is no picker to visually @mention those apps in a chat.</p>
<p id="p188">Some might say that I’m too hard on Apple, and that I should be happy that Siri finally has an app. Let me be clear: I’m very glad that Siri can now be accessed as a persistent application on your Home Screen that lets you keep the same chat going for multiple days. But even if we set aside the more cutting-edge aspects of competing AI services, the new Siri app launching with iOS 27 feels instantly outdated. I expected more after a two-year wait.</p>
<h3 id="visual-intelligence-and-the-siri-camera">Visual Intelligence and the Siri Camera</h3>
<p id="p189">When Visual Intelligence <a href="https://www.macstories.net/stories/apple-intelligence-and-chatgpt-in-18-2/#visual-intelligence" rel="noopener noreferrer">debuted with iOS 18.2</a>, it did so with little fanfare as a hidden feature that required you to long-press the also semi-forgotten Camera Control button to trigger it. Over the past two years, I have never seen anyone in real life use Visual Intelligence, and I think that’s going to change with iOS 27 and Apple’s decision to rebrand it as just “Siri” and put it front and center in the Camera app.</p>
<p id="p190">You can still activate The Feature Formerly Known as Visual Intelligence by long-pressing the Camera Control button, but you don’t have to: just open the camera, then swipe from the default Photo mode to Siri (which has been prominently placed before Portrait mode), and you’ll be able to freeze-frame and start asking questions as before.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/shortcut-upload-1789114676115-a88de15c57.png" alt=""><p class="image-caption"></p></div>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/shortcut-upload-1789114542586-b127b25d95.png" alt="It’s interesting to note what ChatGPT in iOS 26 and Apple Intelligence in iOS 27 thought was the main subject of the picture in Visual Intelligence."><p class="image-caption">It’s interesting to note what ChatGPT in iOS 26 and Apple Intelligence in iOS 27 thought was the main subject of the picture in Visual Intelligence.</p></div>
<aside class="aside aligncenter aside-wide" id="aside193"><h2 id="nutrition-labels-and-bill-splitting">Nutrition Labels and Bill Splitting</h2>
<p>Siri AI in the Camera app can also be used to scan food labels and look up nutritional values, or to scan a receipt and split the bill with friends via Apple Cash.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/14/shortcut-upload-1789386327316-0348c5c8ca.png" alt="Who would have guessed that a [typical Roman pinsa](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pinsa_(bread)) would only be considered “OK”?"><p class="image-caption">Who would have guessed that a <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pinsa_(bread)" rel="noopener noreferrer noopener noreferrer">typical Roman pinsa</a> would only be considered “OK”?</p></div>
<p>While I don’t have access to Apple Cash in Italy, I’ve been able to test nutrition label support with Siri AI, and it worked perfectly – confirming, once again, that the underlying model is inherently multilingual and is only blocked in the EU for regulatory reasons.</p>
</aside><p id="p194">Despite the fresh coat of paint, the underlying mechanics of the feature are the same. You can freeze-frame on any subject, and Siri will give you an automatic response based on what you’re looking at. The Siri camera, as before, supports a variety of data detectors for text, units, and other symbols recognized on screen, and it allows you to either follow up by typing a question or check out related image results with extensions, with Google Images still being the default provider.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/shortcut-upload-1789115063446-3d1170b2cb.png" alt=""><p class="image-caption"></p></div>
<p id="p196">The most important difference from iOS 26 is that answers in the Siri camera will be provided by the new Siri AI, not ChatGPT. And that means that you can integrate this new Siri surface with everything else on your device. A few months ago, our building manager posted a notice with the dates for the mosquito spraying that happens every couple of weeks during the summer. Every year, we want to make sure we remember this is happening so we can close our windows beforehand. When I saw it, I opened the Siri camera, pressed the side button to invoke Siri inside the camera, and asked it to add those dates to our Family calendar. Siri froze the frame, found the dates, and created about a dozen events with the right time inside the calendar that I share with Silvia.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/shortcut-upload-1789115299847-ec3b1ecd6d.png" alt="This kind of interaction is terrific with Siri AI – and it proves that the model is inherently multilingual, even if it’s launching as English-only."><p class="image-caption">This kind of interaction is terrific with Siri AI – and it proves that the model is inherently multilingual, even if it’s launching as English-only.</p></div>
<p id="p198">Earlier this summer, I spent a few days in <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gaeta" rel="noopener noreferrer">Gaeta</a>, and as we were walking to dinner one night, we spotted this huge vessel docked in front of the restaurant. Typically, I would have taken a picture, started a new ChatGPT thread, uploaded the image, and asked a question about it. This time, I simply held down the Camera Control button (which now defaults to Siri AI mode in the Camera app) and captured a photo. A few seconds later, Siri AI told me I was looking at the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Italian_training_ship_Palinuro" rel="noopener noreferrer">Palinuro</a>, a three-masted barquentine that serves as a training vessel for the Italian Navy – which was spot-on. An interaction that would have taken 20-30 seconds with ChatGPT was reduced to a five-second affair with Siri AI. Better still, I was able to ask follow-up questions from Siri AI’s floating overlay as well as find the chat in the Siri app later, where I continued asking questions about the ship’s history and usual locations.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/07/12/shortcut-upload-1783859217868-a90052f45d.png" alt="The ability to go from Siri in the Camera to a longer conversation in the dedicated Siri app is one of my favorite aspects of Siri AI."><p class="image-caption">The ability to go from Siri in the Camera to a longer conversation in the dedicated Siri app is one of my favorite aspects of Siri AI.</p></div>
<p id="p200">These are just two examples of the kind of integration that you can have between the Camera app and Siri, and it would be impossible for me to cover all of them. As you may have noticed with my recent coverage of AI products and features, my job is becoming more about explaining the foundation and harness of AI tools rather than what they can accomplish – because they can achieve almost anything. And so depending on what apps you use and what you need from Siri, perhaps you’ll use the Siri camera to translate a restaurant menu and send it over iMessage to a friend; maybe you’ll want to point the camera at a book and ask Siri to create a note about it in Apple Notes; perhaps you’ll point the camera at a monument in Florence and ask Siri to tell you more about its history. All of these use cases will work, and this AI feature – like everything else – will be what you make of it.</p>
<h3 id="write-with-siri-and-context-menus">Write with Siri and Context Menus</h3>
<p id="p201">There are two more Siri AI surfaces I want to cover. The first one is ‘Ask Siri’, a permanent fixture of every context menu in iOS and iPadOS 27 that lets you instantly pass whatever item you’ve selected – whether it’s a file, some text, a note, or a link – to Siri as input, so you can ask something about it. This isn’t a particularly special feature, but it highlights the kind of pervasive integration Siri AI now has at a system level.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/shortcut-upload-1789115535006-a7fc0ed0a7.png" alt="You can ask Siri anything from anywhere in iOS 27."><p class="image-caption">You can ask Siri anything from anywhere in iOS 27.</p></div>
<p id="p203">The other integration is ‘Write with Siri’, a system-wide replacement for Writing Tools that eschews Writing Tools’ menu and options in favor of a more freeform interaction with Siri.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/shortcut-upload-1789115759126-0ff3efd6cb.png" alt=""><p class="image-caption"></p></div>
<p id="p205">You can access Write with Siri via the Siri button above the keyboard on the left, or by selecting the Siri icon in the action menu when some text is selected. With an active text selection, default ‘Proofread’ and ‘Rewrite’ options will also appear in the QuickType bar.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/shortcut-upload-1789115844712-e4d96a3574.png" alt="You can just do anything with text using the new Siri."><p class="image-caption">You can just do anything with text using the new Siri.</p></div>
<p id="p207">No matter the activation method, there’s no longer a “static” UI for how you want text to be modified or generated: you can just ask Siri. That’s how LLMs work – you ask, they do – so why limit this feature to three writing styles or four formatting options, as it used to be in iOS 26, when you can just prompt the model to write or format text in any way you want?</p>
<p id="p208">Now, I’ve never been a huge Writing Tools fan, so I don’t have a strong opinion about the rebrand in iOS 27, but I think the new design works and makes sense given the stronger abilities of Siri AI. I like that you can compare before/after changes with inline, swipeable cards at the bottom of the screen, and I like that you can ask it to edit anything as long as you’re in a text field. I’m not going to use this much, but for quick typo checks or making an email sound more formal, I find the new feature more versatile and capable than before.</p>
<h3 id="using-siri-ai">Using Siri AI</h3>
<p id="p209">I’ll be honest: I’m not one of those people claiming they have <em>completely</em> stopped using ChatGPT or other agents in favor of Siri AI.</p>
<p id="p210">The new Siri is not an agentic product that is able to carry out long-running tasks to completion, and it would be downright absurd to compare Siri AI to the likes of ChatGPT Work, Claude Cowork, Grok Bot, Hermes Agent, Open Minis, or OpenClaw. I would love for Siri AI to become that kind of experience, and Apple seems to think it now has a foundation in place to enable agentic behavior at some point. But as things stand now, given my reliance on Notion, Slack, and Superhuman for work, and given the simpler, less intelligent nature of Siri AI, I can’t imagine using Apple’s assistant for productivity purposes. In the making of this review, I never used Siri AI once; research work, editing, asset management and video production, database organization, and coding tasks all took place in either Codex with GPT-6 Astra, Claude with Fable 5.1, and Open Minis on iOS.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/shortcut-upload-1789117366922-647431005e.png" alt="This is what I mean by not being able to use Siri AI for work. The same question could not be handled by Siri in Reminders, whereas my agent with Open Minis completed it just fine."><p class="image-caption">This is what I mean by not being able to use Siri AI for work. The same question could not be handled by Siri in Reminders, whereas my agent with Open Minis completed it just fine.</p></div>
<p id="p212">Instead, I’ve come to appreciate Siri AI for what it is – a much better, smarter Siri that doesn’t aim to replace your favorite agent (yet) – without being upset that it’s not something more.</p>
<p id="p213">I’ve thoroughly enjoyed using Siri with Apple Notes, Reminders, HomeKit, and third-party apps such as <a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/askplay-ai-music-assistant/id6760381556" rel="noopener noreferrer">AskPlay</a>, <a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/monologue-smart-dictation/id6755956193" rel="noopener noreferrer">Monologue</a>, <a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/caffeine-tracker-alyx/id6473840226" rel="noopener noreferrer">Alyx</a>, <a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/musicbox-catalog-and-organize/id1614730313" rel="noopener noreferrer">MusicBox</a>, and <a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/lookup-english-dictionary-app/id872564448" rel="noopener noreferrer">LookUp</a>. I’ve found Siri AI to be best at quick, one-off interactions that perhaps involve a couple of follow-ups, but not anything much longer than that. If I realize that a question I have requires deeper research and more back-and-forth, that’s where I fall back to ChatGPT or the excellent <a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/open-minis/id6759188481" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open Minis for iOS</a>.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/shortcut-upload-1789116953688-95427810b0.png" alt="Siri AI integration worked well for me with LookUp, Alyx, and Monologue. It’s also nice to chain output from one app onto another, such as getting a dictation from Monologue and asking Siri to add it to a note."><p class="image-caption">Siri AI integration worked well for me with LookUp, Alyx, and Monologue. It’s also nice to chain output from one app onto another, such as getting a dictation from Monologue and asking Siri to add it to a note.</p></div>
<p id="p215">As I’ve outlined across this entire chapter, there are pros and cons everywhere with Siri AI. Put another way: Apple’s belated effort has paid off, but there’s still so much work to be done. For starters, I think it’s bewildering that Apple hasn’t been able to work with the EU and offer Siri AI in more regions and languages. The fact that Siri AI is still not multilingual is the single reason I still have a ChatGPT Voice shortcut on my Lock Screen, and I continue to believe that if Apple can’t swallow their pride and figure out how to build AI tools for the EU, they will only drive more users to competitors who have learned to work under European regulations.</p>
<aside class="pull-quote aligncenter" id="aside216"><blockquote>
<p>Siri AI is a smarter Siri that doesn’t aim to replace your favorite agent (yet).</p>
</blockquote>
</aside><p id="p217">Second, Siri AI still exhibits old behaviors such as slow performance, requests that time out, or “sorry, something went wrong” messages that aren’t helpful and do not inspire confidence in the product. There are new issues, too. I’ve discovered, for instance, that I can ask Siri to perform two things at the same time – such as setting a timer and turning on a light – but not three. If I ask for three things in the same question, the third one will be silently dropped. Indexing remains a mystery: sometimes I can find messages with Silvia or John from two years ago, but if I ask for something they texted me yesterday, Siri AI can’t find anything.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/shortcut-upload-1789117662839-cacfc7485e.png" alt="If my experience is any indication, expect to see a lot of strange errors involving third-party apps and multiple requests in Siri AI."><p class="image-caption">If my experience is any indication, expect to see a lot of strange errors involving third-party apps and multiple requests in Siri AI.</p></div>
<p id="p219">The system also gets confused with names of third-party apps, which remain my biggest question mark and will need more coverage as more apps add support for Siri AI and App Intents. I was using <a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/focus-deep-work-timer/id975017240" rel="noopener noreferrer">Focus</a>, a timer app, which added support for Siri AI; every time I asked Siri to start a timer in the Focus app, it kept creating a timer labeled ‘Focus’ in the Clock app instead. The model and system orchestrator also appear to exhibit the typical tells of Gemini models, inherited through distillation. Apple’s Foundation models can often be lazy at tool calling and will consider a question answered after doing the bare minimum. In one test I did, I asked whether I had recently been on any trips, and since the <a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/mercury-weather/id1621800675" rel="noopener noreferrer">Mercury Weather</a> app exposes the ability to search for trips, Siri AI defaulted to Mercury Weather, which had no recent trips in my library. Only when I pushed back and added “you have to look in Photos too” did Siri AI realize that I’d indeed been on vacation two weeks before.</p>
<p id="p220">If you’ve ever used Gemini models with external integrations, this kind of behavior may sound familiar. And if you plan on using Siri AI with lots of apps on your device, expect to issue very detailed prompts to get the kind of result you want. Unlike large frontier models, Apple’s models aren’t able to infer user intent unless you are very clear about what you’re seeking.</p>
<p id="p221">Once I understood these limitations, I started having a considerably nicer experience with Siri AI, and I accepted that I shouldn’t expect it to be an alternative to ChatGPT Work or Hermes Agent. Instead, Siri AI is a great “quick answer assistant” that happens to work with native apps and – importantly – can be activated from anywhere and can see my screen. I’ve become a heavy user of on-screen awareness to capture all kinds of things in Notes; I like using Siri AI to quickly summarize articles in Safari; in general, I find myself context-switching between the app I’m using and ChatGPT less because Siri AI can give me a pretty good answer right there.</p>
<p id="p222">ChatGPT is for <em>projects</em>, Siri AI is for quick actions and fast answers. This is how I think about these AI products, and I’m happier now that I’m not trying to force Siri AI into being something it can’t be.</p>
<hr id="hr223"><p id="p224">Siri AI permeates iOS 27, and that’s a threat to OpenAI and the hundreds of millions of people using ChatGPT for free with <a href="https://openai.com/index/gpt-5-5-instant/" rel="noopener noreferrer">GPT-5.5 Instant</a>. I don’t think Apple’s Foundation models are as good as GPT-5.5 Instant, but Siri AI is the more ergonomic and integrated product. When the underlying chatbot and its world knowledge capabilities are <em>good enough</em>, and they can also be invoked via voice, text, the Camera app, context menus, or keyboard shortcuts, why would I open a siloed chatbot when I can use Siri AI and get a comparable answer more comfortably? If you’re a casual user of ChatGPT and do not pay for it, I think Siri AI can be a very compelling product that provides similar answers and, unlike OpenAI’s chatbot, integrates with the iPhone apps you already use. I don’t think it’s a coincidence that OpenAI is now following in Anthropic’s footsteps to monetize the business and enterprise markets rather than the consumer one.</p>
<p id="p225">At the same time, my concern is that we may be collectively assuming that “normal people” won’t see the differences between the capabilities of ChatGPT and Siri AI. In this industry, we have a tendency to sell “normal people” short and paint them as some perplexed bunch of occasional smartphone users who somehow passively accept any technology that is thrown at them. I strongly disagree with that. Maybe those people don’t care about ChatGPT Work, Codex, or Fable (yet?), but they can tell when an AI assistant is useful and when it’s not. People will compare the utility of ChatGPT to Siri AI, and I worry that Apple’s 1.0 product will feel like it’s too little, too late – especially since Siri AI will be launching in select regions and in English only.</p>
<p id="p226">In the AI scene, companies move fast and break things. Apple needs to move fast without breaking anything – not anymore, at least. That’s a tough challenge, but here we are.</p>
<p id="p227"><!--nextpage--></p>
<div class="cover-image cover-image-themed" id="div228">
    <img decoding="async" class="cover-image-content cover-image-dark" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/14/shortcut-upload-1789383922516-6acac23c71.png" alt="" loading="lazy" data-low-fi-src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/14/shortcut-upload-1789383927392-987eddce16.png"><img decoding="async" class="cover-image-content cover-image-light" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/14/shortcut-upload-1789383892463-8a16414489.png" alt="" loading="lazy" data-low-fi-src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/14/shortcut-upload-1789383897710-5a84552ae7.png"></div>
<h2 id="ipados">iPadOS</h2>
<p id="p229">How do you follow up one of the <a href="https://www.macstories.net/stories/ios-and-ipados-26-the-macstories-review/9/#ipados" rel="noopener noreferrer">biggest and most important software updates</a> ever released for the iPad? Unsurprisingly, if you’re Apple, you don’t. Unlike its predecessor and its complete rethinking of multitasking and <a href="https://www.macstories.net/stories/ios-and-ipados-26-the-macstories-review/9/#the-new-windowing" rel="noopener noreferrer">windowing</a>, iPadOS 27 is a smaller release that only introduces a handful of iPad-specific changes and is instead focused on improving performance, tweaking Liquid Glass, and matching the other features introduced on iOS 27, such as Siri AI.</p>
<p id="p230">There is very little in the way of iPad-only functionalities this year, and I don’t think that’s going to shock anyone who’s spent any number of years working on the iPad. Historically, we have gone multiple years between iPad-focused updates – and that was before something as groundbreaking as iPadOS 26. I’m not saying that Apple isn’t capable of shipping major updates year after year; I’m simply arguing that, at this point, we know that the iPad’s typical cadence – including its 18-month refresh cycle for hardware – doesn’t lend itself to major platform updates every September.</p>
<p id="p231">That’s not to say the journey toward making the iPad more like a laptop for professional users has stopped, despite the slower pace. The most relevant addition to iPadOS 27, in fact, is a reworked menu bar that behaves more like its counterpart on macOS now, in a couple of distinct ways. For starters, the name of the foreground app is now always shown in the top-left corner of the menu bar, right next to the date and time.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/shortcut-upload-1789118412661-1686543759.png" alt="The name of the foreground app is now always shown in the menu bar. As you can see, the menu bar is also left-aligned now, just like on macOS."><p class="image-caption">The name of the foreground app is now always shown in the menu bar. As you can see, the menu bar is also left-aligned now, just like on macOS.</p></div>
<p id="p233">In addition to serving as a helpful visual aid for what is currently the active window in the foreground, the app’s name also acts as a pointer target to reveal the full menu bar on hover. Although this interaction was available before in iPadOS 26, I find hovering the pointer over the name of an app a more natural way of revealing the full menu bar than hovering over some empty space at the top and hoping it works.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper video-autoplay js-video-autoplay aligncenter aligncenter aligncenter aligncenter aligncenter" id="div234">
            <video data-autoplay="yes" width="1599" height="464" data-is-retina="yes" preload="auto" playsinline controls><source src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/12/ipad-menu-bar-1789217317470-4cbcb75b6d.mp4" type="video/mp4"><source src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/12/ipad-menu-bar-1789217317470-4cbcb75b6d.webm" type="video/webm"><source src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/12/ipad-menu-bar-1789217317470-4cbcb75b6d.ogv" type="video/ogg"></source></source></source></video><div class="caption">
<p>Revealing the menu bar on hover.</p>
</div></div>
<p id="p235">But that’s not all: you can now make the entire menu bar always visible by default, just like on a Mac. To turn on this option, head over to Settings → Multitasking &amp; Gestures → Automatically Show and Hide Menu Bar and disable the toggle. Now, the menu bar will always be displayed in its expanded state with the classic ‘File, Edit, etc.’ menu items.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/shortcut-upload-1789118433017-37f2a5ecc5.png" alt="The full, left-aligned menu bar in iPadOS 27."><p class="image-caption">The full, left-aligned menu bar in iPadOS 27.</p></div>
<p id="p237">And speaking of those menu items, icons next to menu labels are now used more sparingly than before. This has been a contentious topic <a href="https://daringfireball.net/2026/03/what_to_do_about_those_menu_item_icons_in_macos_26_tahoe" rel="noopener noreferrer">in some parts of the Apple community</a> for the past year, and I don’t have a particular opinion about this since the icons never bothered me <em>that</em> much. That said, I will agree that the menus now look cleaner and less visually busy. But I’ll let John analyze this change in more depth in his Golden Gate review, since much of this debate originated with longtime Mac users. Overall, I’m a fan of the always-visible, left-aligned menu bar because a) it’s more consistent with macOS and b) it makes it easier to discover commands nested inside menus.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/shortcut-upload-1789118452357-ce7eeccd5e.png" alt="Menus with fewer icons."><p class="image-caption">Menus with fewer icons.</p></div>
<p id="p239">There isn’t much else to discuss about iPadOS 27 beyond the menu bar and changes that I’ve already covered in the Design and Siri AI chapters. In terms of iPad-exclusive features, Visual Intelligence has now expanded to the iPad, and it lets you circle elements in screenshots with the Apple Pencil to look them up using Siri AI. It’s very nicely done, but I don’t really use it.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/shortcut-upload-1789118472265-336c54298e.png" alt="Visual Intelligence on iPadOS."><p class="image-caption">Visual Intelligence on iPadOS.</p></div>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/shortcut-upload-1789118508934-9b5c4da290.png" alt=""><p class="image-caption"></p></div>
<p id="p242">As everyone guessed months before the <a href="https://www.macstories.net/news/iphone-18-pro-and-iphone-duo-the-macstories-overview/" rel="noopener noreferrer">introduction of the iPhone Duo</a>, the ability to resize iPhone apps running on the iPad was, in fact, a prelude to iOS apps expanding horizontally on a new form factor. Again, this one isn’t for me, since the apps I use have native iPad versions (essentially every iOS 27 app beta I tested was a Universal app with built-in iPad support). However, if you use an iPhone-only app on your iPad and that app updates for iOS 27, you’ll be able to resize it either on an iPad or in iPhone Mirroring mode on a Mac. Since I believe it’s more useful to do this on a Mac, I’ll let John cover this in his macOS Golden Gate review, too.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/cleanshot-2026-09-11-at-112632-am2x-1789118854075-28389e2524.png" alt="An iPhone app…"><p class="image-caption">An iPhone app…</p></div>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/cleanshot-2026-09-11-at-112644-am2x-1789118858477-795d7b90c6.png" alt="…can now almost become an iPad one when resized."><p class="image-caption">…can now almost become an iPad one when resized.</p></div>
<p id="p245">And to wrap up the list of iPad enhancements that are coming from macOS and will make more sense in the context of John’s review: the sidebars on iPad are no longer “floating” glass panels; they’re edge-to-edge again.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/12/shortcut-upload-1789205717922-0bdb31601d.png" alt="The “new” iPadOS 27 sidebars."><p class="image-caption">The “new” iPadOS 27 sidebars.</p></div>
<p id="p247">This was another heavily debated topic in the Mac community for the past year, and it’s actually a reversal I agree with. Sidebars are supposed to look edge-to-edge like this, and I never fully understood why Apple went with that look in iPadOS and macOS 26. I can’t wait to read where John lands on this one as a Mac user who actually didn’t hate macOS Tahoe as much as others.</p>
<hr id="hr248"><p id="p249">And that’s a wrap on iPad-only changes for this year. I continue to believe that iPadOS would benefit from a stronger Spotlight with the same integrations seen on macOS last year, a clipboard manager, <a href="https://www.macstories.net/notes/faking-clamshell-mode-with-external-displays-in-ipados-17/" rel="noopener noreferrer">proper support for clamshell mode</a> (especially now that Control Center can be invoked on external displays, also new this year!), and – a bigger conversation – a more vibrant app ecosystem <a href="https://www.macstories.net/stories/the-ipads-sweet-solution/" rel="noopener noreferrer">in the age of AI</a>.</p>
<p id="p250">Clearly, though, this was not the year for another iPadOS milestone at Apple. And given the excellent work the company has put into system performance, Liquid Glass improvements, and the launch of Siri AI after several delays, I can’t really blame them for it.</p>
<p id="p251"><!--nextpage--></p>
<div class="cover-image cover-image-themed" id="div252">
    <img decoding="async" class="cover-image-content cover-image-dark" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/14/shortcut-upload-1789384477180-e99dd1e762.png" alt="" loading="lazy" data-low-fi-src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/14/shortcut-upload-1789384480258-c980af37ad.png"><img decoding="async" class="cover-image-content cover-image-light" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/14/shortcut-upload-1789384464595-13c1ea96cf.png" alt="" loading="lazy" data-low-fi-src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/14/shortcut-upload-1789384467962-2f390d9d77.png"></div>
<h2 id="apps">Apps</h2>
<p id="p253">Given the growing number of built-in Apple apps, their cross-platform nature, regional restrictions for certain features, and the simultaneous release of iOS, iPadOS, and macOS 27, we think it’s best to split app coverage between my review and John’s. We <a href="https://www.macstories.net/stories/ios-and-ipados-18-the-macstories-review/6/#apps" rel="noopener noreferrer">debuted</a> this approach two years ago; this year’s reviews will follow the same structure. It makes even more sense this year given Apple’s focus on fixes and smaller tweaks that encompass apps across its entire family of OSes.</p>
<p id="p254">This year, you’ll find the following apps covered in John’s upcoming review of macOS Golden Gate:</p>
<ul id="ul255"><li>Home</li>
<li>Image Playground</li>
<li>iPhone Mirroring</li>
<li>Maps</li>
<li>Passwords</li>
<li>Podcasts</li>
<li>Preview</li>
<li>Sidecar</li>
<li>Weather</li>
</ul><p id="p256">I’ll be covering the rest here.</p>
<h3 id="calendar">Calendar</h3>
<p id="p257">Can you believe it? Fourteen years after <a href="https://www.macstories.net/reviews/fantastical-for-iphone-review/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Fantastical first brought</a> natural language event creation to iOS, when LLMs were a distant dream, Apple’s Calendar app is getting support for “describing events” with natural language in iOS 27. Talk about a “finally” here.</p>
<p id="p258">When typing an event or reminder in the Calendar app, you’ll notice that some parts of the sentence will be highlighted in red to signify that they have been recognized by Apple Intelligence. By “some parts”, I mean the time of an event and the date, but only after you accept a suggestion for a recognized date above the keyboard. When an event gets parsed by AI, you’ll also see an Apple Intelligence symbol next to your typed sentence, and you’ll be able to tweak the event’s title in a separate field.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/shortcut-upload-1789119298221-c6c9cf8bd9.png" alt="Natural language event creation is slightly better than before, but not much."><p class="image-caption">Natural language event creation is slightly better than before, but not much.</p></div>
<p id="p260">Unfortunately, despite being over a decade late to this feature, Apple’s take on natural language event creation is still inferior to Fantastical’s version from 2012. I’m actually astounded by how barebones this functionality is compared to the rest of the industry and other apps that were doing this before AI. Apple’s system does not support specifying calendars or any other metadata about an event. It only works automatically with event times, and if you want a date to be parsed, you need to manually accept a suggestion. I think a Shortcuts user with a decent prompt for the ‘Use Model’ action and the Cloud Pro model could do a better job than this parser.</p>
<p id="p261">For all these reasons, I’m very much let down by natural language event creation in iOS 27, and I hope Apple can ship a real version of this feature next year. They might as well have skipped this.</p>
<h3 id="camera">Camera</h3>
<p id="p262">As happens every September, Apple likes to save some surprises for the Camera app very late in the beta cycle, so they can make new feature announcements coincide with new iPhone reveals. This year <a href="https://www.macstories.net/news/iphone-18-pro-and-iphone-duo-the-macstories-overview/" rel="noopener noreferrer">is no different</a>: as part of the iPhone 18 Pro’s unveiling and its pro photography narrative, Apple also added customizable controls to the Camera app in the Release Candidate version of iOS 27.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/shortcut-upload-1789119446481-41908ae8b2.png" alt="Customizing controls in the Camera app."><p class="image-caption">Customizing controls in the Camera app.</p></div>
<p id="p264">To customize which controls are available for quick access in the Camera app, tap the grid button on the right side of the shutter button, then tap ‘Edit’ in the top-left corner of the viewfinder. Doing so will reveal a good-looking Liquid Glass panel with a list of controls that you can tap to add to the strip at the top of the viewfinder. You can also remove existing ones, or reset everything to the default state if you want to revert your changes.</p>
<p id="p265">Note that control customization is available for all modes of the Camera except for Siri and Panorama.</p>
<h3 id="clock">Clock</h3>
<p id="p266">Morning alarm people, rejoice: in iOS 27, you can now set an independent volume level for alarms that is different from your ringtone volume or other system alerts and sounds.</p>
<p id="p267">(I used to have alarms in the morning, like a regular person. Then, the alarm became my dogs kissing my face because they want to eat. I prefer it.)</p>
<p id="p268">This feature is actually part of a bigger overhaul of volume levels with separate sliders that you can find in Settings → Sounds &amp; Haptics. While everything used to be controlled by the ‘Ringtone &amp; Alerts’ slider in iOS 26, iOS 27 has spun off ‘Alarms and Timers’ and ‘Alerts and System Sounds’ into their own categories, probably because nobody uses ringtones anymore.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/shortcut-upload-1789119521487-831e1b8eee.png" alt="At long last, separate volume sliders."><p class="image-caption">At long last, separate volume sliders.</p></div>
<p id="p270">By default, the new sections will have ‘Match Ringtone Volume’ enabled; if you turn it off, you’ll unlock a separate volume slider for those features.</p>
<p id="p271">I keep my iPhone permanently on mute, so this feature isn’t for me, but, as always, I am glad to see more customization everywhere.</p>
<h3 id="files">Files</h3>
<p id="p272">There are only two improvements to the Files app worth noting this year.</p>
<p id="p273">The first one is for iPad users: if you transfer large files to external drives on a regular basis, you should notice that those copies will be up to five times faster than in iPadOS 26. Apple claims that this change makes the Files app “just as fast as Finder on the Mac”, and my understanding is that these tests were conducted with APFS-formatted drives over a USB4 connection. I do not need to transfer large files from my iPad Pro to an external drive anymore (I now record podcasts on my Mac via Google Chrome and Riverside), so I’m looking forward to seeing what my friend <a href="https://www.youtube.com/channel/UC8raOG7HXJoCUygx219fU4A" rel="noopener noreferrer">Chris</a> has to say about this.</p>
<p id="p274">Second: Files now supports AI-powered renaming of files and folders. I like this feature a lot. When you press ‘Rename’ on a file or folder and wait a couple of seconds, you’ll see some naming suggestions appear above the keyboard. Choose one, and the file or folder will be renamed instantly.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/shortcut-upload-1789119648244-50c06176de.png" alt="AI-based file renaming works pretty well."><p class="image-caption">AI-based file renaming works pretty well.</p></div>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/12/shortcut-upload-1789196059687-846bd52b48.png" alt="AI-assisted renaming also works when saving a document via the Files picker. Clarus was, in fact, recognized as a “dog”."><p class="image-caption">AI-assisted renaming also works when saving a document via the Files picker. Clarus was, in fact, recognized as a “dog”.</p></div>
<p id="p277">It’s interesting to observe how this feature works behind the scenes. The model powering this appears to be using a mix of file analysis and vision capabilities to suggest names. The model can suggest a cleaner title for a PDF by reading it, or a more descriptive name for a folder based on the items inside it, but it’ll also attempt to inspect an image with vision and offer a new name. For instance, I had a file in iCloud Drive called <code>Codex.png</code> for the Codex icon, and when I tried to rename it, the Files app offered “Blue Cloud” as a suggestion.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/shortcut-upload-1789119760481-3ddf6ad508.png" alt=""><p class="image-caption"></p></div>
<p id="p279">That’s not necessarily better than the actual filename I had, but it confirmed that the model is analyzing images before offering filename suggestions. Amusingly, when I tried to rename a photo of Liam and Noel Gallagher, the system came up with “Two People” as the name, but when I tried with a photo of my dog sleeping, it didn’t work.</p>
<p id="p280">For science, I also tried to prompt-inject this feature by leaving some instructions inside a text document I wanted to rename, and the model ignored them. Good job, Apple.</p>
<h3 id="find-my">Find My</h3>
<p id="p281">Continuing Apple’s streak of smaller additions throughout the OS this year, the Find My app has received a new option for location sharing and better support for pinpointing your location.</p>
<p id="p282">When sharing your location with someone, you can now choose a ‘Custom’ option that will bring up a date and time picker to share your location for an exact amount of time or until a specific date and time. This is great for conferences or vacations, for example, where you may want to share your location with other people but have it expire automatically at a specific time. It also works for quick sessions – the minimum duration is 15 minutes.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/shortcut-upload-1789119845593-b7de9ace9a.png" alt="New location sharing options in Find My."><p class="image-caption">New location sharing options in Find My.</p></div>
<p id="p284">Additionally, in iOS 27, location sharing automatically picks the right device: your iPhone if it has an active phone number, or your Apple Watch if it’s a GPS + Cellular model.</p>
<p id="p285"><!--nextpage--></p>
<h3 id="journal">Journal</h3>
<p id="p286">I’ve long been the kind of person who wants to keep a journal, and I’ve never been able to stick to it. For a while now, I’ve thought that my problem is that I’m a writer by trade: after I spend all day writing and talking, I don’t want to sit down at the end of it and write some more about myself.</p>
<p id="p287">This is why the AI integrations in the Journal app for iOS and iPadOS 27 are especially interesting for someone like me. This year, you’ll find a new ‘Text’ icon above the keyboard (next to the smart journaling suggestions) that opens a panel of writing prompts. If you get stuck while journaling about your day or a particular activity, Apple Intelligence can help you keep going by asking questions related to what you’ve written so far. You can pick a question, which will be added to the journal entry in purple text, and then continue writing below it.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/shortcut-upload-1789119979889-246f48441d.png" alt="Generative prompts in Journal."><p class="image-caption">Generative prompts in Journal.</p></div>
<p id="p289">As with all generative text – and especially text generated by models that are not state-of-the-art in 2026 – some suggestions can be pretty good, others are kind of creepy, and some are just nonsensical. When I journaled about trying to finish this review in time, Apple Intelligence asked me if I felt stressed or how the rush would impact my day, and that was pretty good. But when I added a screenshot of my review workspace in Notion that had a pencil emoji, Apple Intelligence asked me if there was a deeper meaning behind it. And for another entry with separate photos of my dogs sleeping, Apple Intelligence – and I’m quoting this insanity below – asked:</p>
<blockquote id="blockquote290"><p>
  What stories could emerge from the contrast between the two resting positions?
</p></blockquote>
<p id="p291">Buddy, they’re two pictures of my dogs sleeping. Don’t try so hard.</p>
<p id="p292">I’ll also note that journal entries now show timestamps in addition to dates, which I’m very surprised was not the case before.</p>
<h3 id="mail">Mail</h3>
<p id="p293">I can’t believe I’m typing this, but Apple fixed Mail search in iOS 27 thanks to the new indexing behind Siri AI, and I’m now at the point where I may seriously consider switching to Apple Mail again. From my perspective, this is the biggest plot twist of the app story in iOS 27.</p>
<p id="p294">As part of this year’s focus on system-wide performance improvements and the new indexing that powers Spotlight and Siri AI, Apple completely re-engineered the Mail app’s architecture to support faster message loading and vastly improved search that finds relevant messages more quickly, with optional support for natural language queries. This has been a recurring frustration across my entire history of iOS reviews: Apple Mail’s search was never good enough. It was slow, it could never find what I was looking for, and as soon as AI integrations for Gmail started coming out two years ago, it seemed like I would be done with Apple Mail forever and use third-party services instead.</p>
<p id="p295">Lo and behold, they actually did it with iOS 27. The new indexing works and the search is great – it’s as simple as that. I’ve been able to find messages from 11 years ago in just a few seconds, and the app’s improved search suggestions do a nice job of highlighting the most relevant and recent messages from a specific contact while still showing you historical results from previous correspondence. And it is <em>fast</em>: on my iPhone 17 Pro, results from a decade ago appear as I type, and when I open an old message that I would assume is not in the system’s cache, it loads in less than two seconds.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/shortcut-upload-1789120801706-03123d8053.png" alt="Mail search actually works in iOS 27."><p class="image-caption">Mail search actually works in iOS 27.</p></div>
<p id="p297">Even nicer: you don’t have to phrase your search queries too precisely to find messages. Thanks to Mail’s new ranking system and Apple Intelligence, the system will know what you mean if you type a sentence such as “Email from Myke in 2013”. Trying to search with this query in iOS 26 would return no results, whereas iOS 27 immediately found my first email conversation with Myke from 13 years ago.</p>
<p id="p298">This all comes together nicely when you combine the improved indexing and ranking of Mail with Siri AI. A few days ago, I asked Siri to find all emails from <a href="https://search.google.com/search-console/about" rel="noopener noreferrer">Google Search Console</a> in my inbox and archive them. In less than 10 seconds, Siri AI asked me to confirm that I wanted to archive 40 messages and displayed a visual snippet. That was it. This kind of request would have taken at least a minute with a frontier model that would have pinged a Gmail connector and reasoned through the steps; Apple’s hybrid orchestration system did it in under 10 seconds because the data had already been indexed on-device. I’ve had similar positive experiences with Mail and Siri AI when searching for specific threads, asking to summarize the latest message in a thread, and more. Alongside Notes and Reminders, Mail makes for one of the best demos of Siri AI to date.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/shortcut-upload-1789120891979-044b8a1307.png" alt="Siri AI is great at taking actions on multiple messages in Mail."><p class="image-caption">Siri AI is great at taking actions on multiple messages in Mail.</p></div>
<p id="p300">The Apple Mail engineers responsible for these improvements honestly deserve a round of applause this year. You know me: when I dislike something, I don’t mince words, but when I like something, I’m not shy about that either. As things stand in iOS 27, I genuinely believe Apple went from having the worst search of any native email client on iPhone and iPad to the best, if you consider privacy, speed, on-device processing, and just how nice everything looks with Liquid Glass. A plot twist, indeed.</p>
<p id="p301">There are some other additions to the Mail app worth mentioning. App integrations are making their debut in the form of the same suggestions seen in Messages, which I will cover later. If you’ve enabled order tracking via Wallet, the integration in Mail will now show you links to manage or track an order in Safari in addition to opening it in Wallet.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/shortcut-upload-1789120727864-6dd06dc568.png" alt="New order tracking options in Mail."><p class="image-caption">New order tracking options in Mail.</p></div>
<p id="p303">Lastly, Apple Mail now also integrates with ‘Write with Siri’, and the system supports composing and editing messages in your own style based on context. I don’t like doing this personally, but I think it’ll be a popular feature with lots of users.</p>
<p id="p304">I did not expect a comeback story with Apple Mail in iOS 27. If you haven’t played with Apple’s email app in a while, now’s the time to take it for a spin again. Just let it index for a few days first.</p>
<h3 id="messages">Messages</h3>
<p id="p305">Much to my surprise, the Messages app is having a relatively quiet year with iOS 27. As with the rest of the system, there are small improvements across the app, but there’s no single “big” feature in Messages for the first time in a while.</p>
<p id="p306">Let’s begin with Justin Bieber having mastered the art of filing feedback with Apple. Everyone knows that the best way to effect change is to complain loudly about an iOS feature on social media, which Bieber <a href="https://www.instagram.com/lilbieber/p/DR3EK3OkmOF/" rel="noopener noreferrer">eloquently did last year</a> when he voiced his keyboard dictation concerns on Instagram.</p>
<p id="p307">In iOS 27, you now have the option to hide the dictation button from the Messages text field so that you, or Mr. Bieber, won’t accidentally press it anymore. To do this, head over to Settings → Messages → Show in Text Field, and you’ll get options to show a button to record an audio message, start dictation, or – finally – ‘None’.</p>
<p id="p308">Not only is this a welcome tweak to the compose experience in iMessage, but we should all be relieved that Mr. Bieber won’t get to put John Ternus in “a rear naked choke hold”. The new Apple CEO lives to fight another day.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/shortcut-upload-1789121259450-29f5a1d79c.png" alt="Thanks, Bieber."><p class="image-caption">Thanks, Bieber.</p></div>
<p id="p310">Moving on to non-threatening feature additions to iOS 27’s Messages app, Apple has now consolidated Tapback reactions in notifications. When multiple people in a group chat send the same reaction to a message, instead of getting separate alerts for them, the system will coalesce them in the same notification. It’s a small thing, but it’s nice to have.</p>
<p id="p311">Lastly, the Drawing app. This is a new app in the iMessage app drawer that lets you draw with native <a href="https://developer.apple.com/documentation/paperkit" rel="noopener noreferrer">PaperKit</a> tools (the same ones seen in Notes and Freeform, which third-party developers can now use too) to send drawings to your friends.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/shortcut-upload-1789121340927-993167da01.png" alt="Sure."><p class="image-caption">Sure.</p></div>
<p id="p313">Why does this exist? Who at Apple is constantly sketching in iMessage and pushed for this?</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/shortcut-upload-1789121412182-10f9b1e9e0.png" alt="*“This is it. You send your heartbeat. It is racing, thumping. Your crush sends their heartbeat back.”*"><p class="image-caption"><em>“This is it. You send your heartbeat. It is racing, thumping. Your crush sends their heartbeat back.”</em></p></div>
<p id="p315">And more importantly: why does Digital Touch also still exist?</p>
<h3 id="music">Music</h3>
<p id="p316">As the kids say these days, Apple Music continues to “mog” Spotify in the design department, and the iOS 27 version is so much better-looking than Spotify for iPhone, it’s not even funny anymore.</p>
<p id="p317">In iOS 27, Apple has redesigned artist pages with extended artwork whose color bleeds through the entire page, and put buttons to read an artist’s bio, play their top songs, and favorite them front and center. The result is dramatic and makes the old Music app feel like it was designed a decade ago.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/shortcut-upload-1789121838667-1930d8af85.png" alt="In addition to redesigned artist pages, Apple is also letting artists upload their own custom logos, as seen with Mayday Parade here."><p class="image-caption">In addition to redesigned artist pages, Apple is also letting artists upload their own custom logos, as seen with Mayday Parade here.</p></div>
<p id="p319">I highly recommend taking a tour of different artist pages in Apple Music and looking at the enhanced art and motion effects the company has sprinkled throughout the catalog. No music streaming service looks as nice as Apple Music does, especially in iOS 27 – not to mention the app’s state-of-the-art real-time lyrics support, which continues to be one of my key reasons for not using Spotify.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/shortcut-upload-1789121689868-7a8ad2d857.png" alt="I mean, this is outstanding design."><p class="image-caption">I mean, this is outstanding design.</p></div>
<p id="p321">Also new in iOS 27: there’s landscape support in the Now Playing screen on iPhone (we all know why this was added in a bunch of system apps this year) as well as improved transitions for AutoMix, which I don’t use because I’m too old for it.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/shortcut-upload-1789121751242-faaa094b02.png" alt="This is so good on a MagSafe charger."><p class="image-caption">This is so good on a MagSafe charger.</p></div>
<p id="p323">The one blemish of the Music app in iOS 27 is, unfortunately, its Siri AI integration. I don’t know if the Music or Siri team is to blame here, but conversational music playback and search with Siri AI is a far cry from the outstanding experience of Apple Mail. While Siri AI can play specific albums or playlists in your library quite reliably, the same can’t be said for searching Apple Music or extracting information from the service’s catalog. When I asked to play some of Oasis’ ‘Live 25’ songs, Siri AI queued 25 songs from Oasis instead, and that tells you everything you need to know.</p>
<p id="p324">I hope to see much deeper integration between Apple Music and Siri AI in iOS 28.</p>
<p id="p325"><!--nextpage--></p>
<h3 id="notes">Notes</h3>
<p id="p326">While working on NotesCTL, <a href="https://www.macstories.net/news/notesctl-a-club-macstories-preview-of-federicos-advanced-apple-notes-cli-tool/" rel="noopener noreferrer">my upcoming Apple Notes CLI for people and AI agents</a>, I’ve been using the Notes app more as a capture system that sits in front of my intricate Notion workspace. I’ve long regarded the modern Notes app as one of the best of Apple’s built-in apps for iPhone and iPad, to the point where, over the summer, I even considered writing this entire review in Notes.</p>
<p id="p327">In iOS and iPadOS 27, in addition to excellent integration with Siri AI, Notes is getting some smaller improvements that, while not groundbreaking, will make power users happy. Perhaps they’ll even convince some of them to switch back from other note-taking apps.</p>
<p id="p328">Let’s start with the smaller things. In Notes’ action menu (open the copy and paste menu, then swipe left), you’ll find a new ‘Insert Divider Line’ option that, well, inserts a line in the current note.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/shortcut-upload-1789121988999-eba7da4947.png" alt=""><p class="image-caption"></p></div>
<p id="p330">If you’ve ever worked on longer notes containing multiple sections, you know that this is actually a nice thing to have. Interestingly, divider lines are also backward compatible with devices running iOS 26: on those devices, divider lines will render as three dashes (<code>---</code>).</p>
<p id="p331">The ability to deeplink to a specific note inside the Notes app, <a href="https://www.macstories.net/stories/ios-and-ipados-17-the-macstories-review/10/#internal-linking-between-notes" rel="noopener noreferrer">introduced in iOS 17</a>, is expanding in iOS 27 with support for linking specific sections of a note as well. As always, you can create an internal link to a note either via the ‘Add Link’ menu or by typing <code>&gt;&gt;</code> to bring up the inline note-linking tool. After selecting a note in the quick menu, if the note contains sections (headings), you can hit <code>/</code> to drill down into the contents of the note and select a particular section you want to link to.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/shortcut-upload-1789122116416-d7538e1ab9.png" alt="Creating section links in Notes."><p class="image-caption">Creating section links in Notes.</p></div>
<p id="p333">When creating section links from the full-blown ‘Add Link’ menu, you’ll find a dedicated ‘Link to Section’ page that lets you preview and navigate all sections contained within the note:</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/shortcut-upload-1789122205726-fab27925b5.png" alt=""><p class="image-caption"></p></div>
<p id="p335">As with divider lines, these links cannot be created on iOS 26, but they are backward compatible with it. When you tap one of these links, you’ll be taken directly to the linked section of the destination note, which will be briefly highlighted on screen. This is a fantastic improvement to the Notes app, and it’ll make it possible for folks to create rich “table of contents” notes and Notion-like dashboards that cross-link to specific sections of other notes in the app. I love it.</p>
<p id="p336">Then, there’s the feature I did not expect to see in the Notes app, ever: integration with <a href="https://daringfireball.net/projects/markdown/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Markdown</a>.</p>
<p id="p337">Support for the popular plain-text formatting standard has been implemented by Apple in a couple of different ways. You can export an entire note as Markdown from the share sheet. Doing this will convert every element of a note to Markdown, including tables and colored text, which makes Apple’s flavor of Markdown compatible with popular forks such as <a href="https://fletcherpenney.net/multimarkdown/" rel="noopener noreferrer">MultiMarkdown</a> and <a href="https://github.github.com/gfm/" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub Flavored Markdown</a>. Even more interesting, however, is the fact that Apple Notes can export notes with attachments, which will be stored in an <code>/Attachments</code> subfolder and referenced within the Markdown text as an inline link. This is what I mean:</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/shortcut-upload-1789122409585-fa0a8b2083.png" alt="Exporting Markdown from Notes preserves local attachments, too."><p class="image-caption">Exporting Markdown from Notes preserves local attachments, too.</p></div>
<p id="p339">This is a solid decision: it means that you can now export a rich-text note from Apple Notes, grab the resulting folder, load it up in apps like <a href="https://ia.net/writer" rel="noopener noreferrer">iA Writer</a> or <a href="https://ulysses.app/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Ulysses</a>, and every reference to every attachment will load correctly. Even Apple Pencil drawings survive the Markdown export: they will be rendered as high-fidelity PNGs and stored in the Attachments folder. At this point, nothing is stopping you from creating a shortcut that takes a rich-text note from the Notes app, uploads images from the Attachments folder to a CDN, replaces the local references with a public HTTPS link, and gets that Markdown ready for publishing on a blog. I’m just saying.</p>
<aside class="info-box " id="aside340"><p>You can also import Markdown text into the Notes app using Shortcuts. In both the ‘Create Note’ and ‘Append to Note’ actions, Apple has added an ‘Interpret as Markdown’ toggle that will render the text passed as input as rich text in Notes. In my tests, this has worked remarkably well, with the only unsupported elements being footnotes and inline HTTPS images, which will not be downloaded and embedded in a note.</p>
<p class="icon"><svg viewbox="0 0 120 120" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><circle clip-rule="evenodd" cx="60" cy="60" fill-rule="evenodd" r="60"></circle><path d="m44 89v-5h13.2v-28.6h-13.1v-5h19.1v33.6h12.8v5zm10.8-53.1c0-2.8 2.2-4.9 5-4.9s5 2.1 5 4.9-2.2 5-5 5-5-2.1-5-5z" fill="#fff"></path></svg></p>
</aside><p id="p341">I continue to be amazed by the progress that Apple has made in the Notes app in recent years. The app can now be used as a serious productivity powerhouse, which is why I’m building NotesCTL and look forward to releasing it soon.</p>
<p id="p342">I wouldn’t put it past Apple to copy Notion and add support for databases and properties to the Notes app. Stranger things have happened to this app, which is way more powerful than you’d expect given its humble origins nearly two decades ago.</p>
<h3 id="phone">Phone</h3>
<p id="p343">The one addition to the Phone app this year is Call Context, which is another enhancement powered by Apple Intelligence. This feature is designed to surface relevant information about a contact or business while you’re on the phone with them; in my limited experience with this, it does work as advertised.</p>
<p id="p344">According to Apple, this feature will bring up reservation codes and confirmation numbers while you’re on a call with a business, but since I fundamentally dislike jumping on a phone call with hotels or airlines and prefer email or WhatsApp, I have not been able to test this.</p>
<p id="p345">Call Context did work when I was on the phone with a contact whose birthday it was. Mind you, the contact was <em>my mom</em>, so I didn’t really need a reminder. Regardless, I thought it was nice that the Phone app embedded a birthday popup and cute animation in the call screen, and I imagine this could be useful if you’re on a call with an acquaintance or a distant relative and want to pretend you remembered it was their birthday all along.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/contact-birthday-when-calling-with-phone-app-1789122528314-5f6c7c13c4.png" alt=""><p class="image-caption"></p></div>
<p id="p347">They’ll never know. Apple Intelligence will.</p>
<h3 id="photos">Photos</h3>
<p id="p348">There are several additions to the Photos app this year, which can be categorized into three buckets that encapsulate iOS 27 as a whole: small quality-of-life features, performance improvements, and AI. Personally, I really like the first two categories, and I strongly dislike the AI features added to Photos; I find them pretty gross.</p>
<p id="p349">The Photos app is much faster than before, with new photos and videos loading up to 70% faster after they’ve been captured. I haven’t verified these claims with a stopwatch, but, anecdotally, the claim holds up. I took a lot of photos and videos this summer, and as soon as I opened the Photos app, the thumbnails were already there and I could open each item to view it in full screen immediately.</p>
<p id="p350">Apple’s focus on performance has also paid off in the search index, with up-to-date results that surface faster than before. To make search even more personal, you can now add custom keywords to photos and videos. To do this, swipe up on a photo to open the inspector, scroll to the bottom, and you’ll find a text field that lets you enter multi-word keywords. These keywords will show up in search results immediately, and they’re a nice way to find items with specific words that may not be supported by Apple’s search engine by default.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/shortcut-upload-1789122652616-2025efbb07.png" alt="Adding keywords to photos in iOS 27."><p class="image-caption">Adding keywords to photos in iOS 27.</p></div>
<p id="p352">Speaking of photos showing up immediately: iOS 27 also brings a new option to prioritize iCloud sync for the rest of the day if you want to make sure that items upload to iCloud right away. To enable this, go to Collections, tap your profile picture at the top, then choose ‘Sync Immediately’ and set it to ‘For Today’. As Apple explains, this feature will affect performance and will be automatically disabled when Low Power Mode or Low Data Mode is turned on; in my experience, it’s been a great time-saver when working on this review to ensure that screenshots would sync across my devices as soon as I took them.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/shortcut-upload-1789122690900-d86936b8a6.png" alt=""><p class="image-caption"></p></div>
<p id="p354">The smaller tweaks to Photos are my favorites this year. I have to start with one of the all-time greats returning to iOS: <strong>the Camera Roll is back</strong>. Well, sort of. In iOS 27, if you want to just see photos and videos you’ve taken and nothing else (no screenshots, no downloaded images from the web, etc.), you can press the filter button at the top of the screen and choose ‘Captured by Me’. Doing this will filter the main view of the Photos app to what it used to be: a beautiful grid of just your photos and videos.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/shortcut-upload-1789122903525-69a7be8c8f.png" alt="Finally, a Camera Roll."><p class="image-caption">Finally, a Camera Roll.</p></div>
<p id="p356">This feature is <em>awesome</em>, but I’m bummed that it can’t be made a default view option of the app. I want Photos to always load like this; instead, when you quit the app and reopen it, you’ll have to re-apply the filter manually. Since this is one of the best “hidden” features of iOS 27, I feel pretty confident that lots of people will ask for it, and Apple will add a way to make the original Camera Roll the default view, in all its glory.</p>
<aside class="info-box wish-box " id="aside357"><p>In fact, I’d even wish for the opposite: let me filter the Photos library by what is <em>not</em> captured by me, and allow me to select everything in it and wipe it from existence. My Photos library is full of junk at this point, but filtering by screenshots or screen recordings alone does not suffice.</p>
<p class="icon"><svg viewbox="0 0 120 120" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path id="Path" fill="currentColor" fill-rule="evenodd" stroke="none" d="M 120 60 C 120 93.137085 93.137085 120 60 120 C 26.862915 120 0 93.137085 0 60 C -0 26.862915 26.862915 0 60 0 C 93.137085 0 120 26.862915 120 60 Z"></path><path id="wish" fill="#fff" stroke="none" d="M 56.3125 44.652519 L 57.90625 41.465019 L 61.09375 39.871269 L 57.90625 38.277519 L 56.3125 35.090019 L 54.71875 38.277519 L 51.53125 39.871269 L 54.71875 41.465019 L 56.3125 44.652519 Z M 41.96875 51.027519 L 44.628319 45.718338 L 49.9375 43.058769 L 44.628319 40.399193 L 41.96875 35.090019 L 39.309181 40.399193 L 34 43.058769 L 39.309181 45.718338 L 41.96875 51.027519 Z M 77.03125 63.777515 L 74.371681 69.0867 L 69.0625 71.746262 L 74.371681 74.405838 L 77.03125 79.715012 L 79.690819 74.405838 L 85 71.746262 L 79.690819 69.0867 L 77.03125 63.777515 Z M 84.063675 44.473221 L 75.616798 36.026344 C 74.989258 35.398804 74.172462 35.090019 73.365623 35.090019 C 72.548828 35.090019 71.732033 35.398804 71.114456 36.026344 L 34.936329 72.194504 C 33.691212 73.439621 33.691212 75.4617 34.936329 76.706818 L 43.393166 85.163651 C 44.020702 85.791183 44.827538 86.099976 45.644337 86.099976 C 46.461132 86.099976 47.277931 85.791183 47.895508 85.163651 L 84.063675 48.985527 C 85.308784 47.74041 85.308784 45.718338 84.063675 44.483177 Z M 69.80957 55.360527 L 64.739456 50.290405 L 73.365623 41.664238 L 78.435745 46.734352 L 69.80957 55.360527 Z"></path></svg></p>
</aside><p id="p358">Another longstanding limitation of Photos that’s been addressed in iOS 27 is the inability to save frames of videos as images. How many times have you had to quickly and precisely take a screenshot of a video to freeze an exact frame from it? No more. In iOS 27, you can simply pause a video, press the ‘…’ button in the top-right corner, and choose ‘Save Video Frame as Photo’. Unlike screenshots, the resulting image will be saved at full resolution with metadata from the original video. This is exactly what I wanted Apple to do, and I have no notes.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/shortcut-upload-1789122999483-f97e5c81a3.png" alt=""><p class="image-caption"></p></div>
<p id="p360">And how often have you ended up taking a picture of your passport or driver’s license again because you knew you had one in Photos, but you couldn’t find it? This has also been solved in iOS 27 with the new ‘Identity Documents’ collection. This section aggregates all pictures of IDs you’ve taken over the years. It’s not perfect since iOS 27 recognized Volume 1 of my <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berserk_(manga)" rel="noopener noreferrer">Berserk</a> collection as a government ID but, hey, who hasn’t wished to be <a href="https://berserk.fandom.com/wiki/Guts" rel="noopener noreferrer">Guts</a> every once in a while?</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/shortcut-upload-1789122874111-3d1a9a9cdc.png" alt=""><p class="image-caption"></p></div>
<p id="p362">I’ve never been into star ratings, but they’ve found their way to Photos for iPhone and iPad. I’m fully aware that some people like to meticulously rate their music and photos, and because of that, I think Apple is doing the right thing in bringing the feature to the Photos app. After enabling this feature in the redesigned settings screen for Photos under Settings → Apps → Photos → View Options → Rating Controls, you’ll see a star badge appear at the top right of a photo when viewing it in full screen. You can tap that to give the photo a star rating, which can later be used as a filter in the main Library tab. Since I’d give all photos of my dogs five stars anyway, I wouldn’t know how to properly take advantage of this.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/shortcut-upload-1789123052264-78fcd2143e.png" alt="I think there’s a bug with this feature? All my dog photos are getting five stars."><p class="image-caption">I think there’s a bug with this feature? All my dog photos are getting five stars.</p></div>
<p id="p364">Another feature that I never use is shared albums (not to mention the shared library), but I should note that they’re getting some welcome enhancements in iOS 27. iCloud shared albums now support full-resolution sharing (imagine my surprise when I learned that it wasn’t the case already), and they can be set to expire after 30 days, giving other people a chance to get those photos after, say, a vacation, without cluttering your library forever.</p>
<p id="p365">Which brings me to the three Apple Intelligence features added to the Photos app: Spatial Reframing, Extend, and Clean Up. I find the first two kind of gross and useless (and I’ll explain why shortly); the third one already exists in iOS 26, but it’s been improved in iOS 27 with a ‘High Quality’ mode for detailed edits.</p>
<p id="p366">The new Clean Up, like the other AI features, can be accessed from the new Tools menu (the one with an Apple Intelligence logo) when viewing a photo. After you select it, you’ll see a loading bar while the model used by Clean Up is prepared. Clean Up defaults to an auto mode, which, as the name implies, automatically determines whether it should use a “fast” version of Clean Up for quick touch-ups or a “high quality” one for detailed work.</p>
<p id="p367">Objectively speaking, Apple’s claims that Clean Up has been overhauled in iOS 27 thanks to the new Foundation models hold up. When I tried to edit a photo of my dog and brushed over a flower vase in the background, iOS 26’s Clean Up removed the entire dresser the vase was on top of, and it left a smudgy mess in the picture. With the same gesture using iOS 27’s high-quality Clean Up, only the vase was removed.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/shortcut-upload-1789123116056-d0871dda58.png" alt=""><p class="image-caption"></p></div>
<p id="p369">See, I like Clean Up, and I use it frequently. So why don’t I like Spatial Reframing and Extend? Simple: while Clean Up is about touch-ups that remove small details of a photo without fundamentally altering its nature, I find generative features such as Apple’s Spatial Reframing and Extend, or what Google does on Pixel phones, to be slop generation machines for my memories.</p>
<p id="p370">Extend is a generative fill that uses a diffusion model to imagine pixels outside the bounds of an original photo. It just makes up what it “thinks” may have been around a subject in a photo, thereby altering a memory with whole scenery, structures, or objects that simply did not exist. Spatial Reframing is even stranger: it lets you adjust the composition and framing of a photo by twisting and rotating subjects, thus also imagining parts of an image that were never captured by you.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/shortcut-upload-1789123321861-39ad2ec7f6.png" alt="Oh no."><p class="image-caption">Oh no.</p></div>
<p id="p372">Although I use AI for so many things in my life, generative applications of it for images, music, creative writing, and, yes, memories aren’t areas I’m remotely interested in. I understand why Apple feels the pressure to add these options to their apps now given the competition and their marketing campaigns, but you won’t find a detailed explanation of these features in my review.</p>
<p id="p373"><!--nextpage--></p>
<h3 id="reminders">Reminders</h3>
<p id="p374">The Reminders app – which is my primary task manager thanks to <a href="https://www.macstories.net/stories/introducing-remctl-the-power-user-reminders-cli-for-macos-and-ai-agents/" rel="noopener noreferrer">my own Reminders CLI</a> – is getting some very minor updates in iOS 27.</p>
<p id="p375">The natural language creation I described in the Calendar section is also in Reminders, and it’s just as bad there. If you’re looking for an excellent natural language utility for fast task creation on iPhone and iPad, I can’t recommend <a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/remind-me-faster/id985555908" rel="noopener noreferrer">Remind Me Faster </a>enough.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/shortcut-upload-1789123475361-2868ad4643.png" alt="Remind Me Faster is still the best natural language entry utility for Reminders on all Apple platforms."><p class="image-caption">Remind Me Faster is still the best natural language entry utility for Reminders on all Apple platforms.</p></div>
<p id="p377">There’s a redesigned inline metadata editor: when you tap a reminder, the old toolbar above the keyboard has been replaced by small buttons at the bottom of the selected item’s card, which include more options than before. Alas, there is still no shortcut to quickly change the list a reminder belongs to.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/shortcut-upload-1789123581844-e7283caa71.png" alt=""><p class="image-caption"></p></div>
<p id="p379">Lastly, Apple claims iOS 27 features improved organization for items in grocery lists, but since I use my iPhone in English and live in Italy, I haven’t been able to test this for myself. (If you use Reminders in English, groceries in other languages won’t auto-organize.)</p>
<h3 id="safari">Safari</h3>
<p id="p380">Aside from <a href="https://webkit.org/blog/17967/news-from-wwdc26-webkit-in-safari-27-beta/" rel="noopener noreferrer">technical improvements to WebKit</a>, which are beyond the scope of this review, there are three additions to Safari for iOS and iPadOS 27: AI-powered tab organization, AI-powered extension creation, and the ability to get notified when content on a website changes.</p>
<p id="p381">I’ll start with the last one, which has never worked for me in my testing over the summer. With the new ‘Notify Me’ feature of Safari, you can describe a change you’d like Safari to look for on any particular webpage, and set a frequency: hourly, daily, weekly, or monthly. Once you’ve described the change and set a schedule, Safari will start monitoring the selected page, and, in theory, should notify you when the change is detected.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/shortcut-upload-1789123645482-fb435a022b.png" alt=""><p class="image-caption"></p></div>
<p id="p383">I’ve tried to test this feature with some blogs I follow by asking Safari to watch for “new articles posted”, and I’ve never been notified about any of them. I like the principle behind this addition: by using a combination of AI and Safari in the background, iOS can let you know if you’re missing out on any important new information added to, say, your kids’ school dashboard or a particular item that went on sale. I just hope Apple can improve the heuristics behind it so that it starts working for different types of websites. Your mileage, of course, may vary.</p>
<aside class="aside aligncenter aside-wide" id="aside384"><h2 id="wheres-the-share-button">Where’s the Share Button?</h2>
<p>In iOS 27, Apple tweaked the design of Safari’s compact tab bar mode (<a href="https://www.macstories.net/stories/ios-and-ipados-26-the-macstories-review/16/#safari" rel="noopener noreferrer noopener noreferrer">introduced</a> last year) with a subtle change: the bottom-right button is no longer a generic ellipsis menu – it opens your tabs instead.</p>
<p>I can’t tell you how happy this change alone makes me. Part of the reason I stopped using compact tab bar mode was that I found it too cumbersome to get back to my open tabs. Yes, I know that I could have swiped up on the address bar to return to the tab overview, but I’ve always found that gesture to require too much precision. The new button is much better: with a single tap I can find all my open tabs without giving up the convenience of a more compact layout that lets me see more content on any webpage.</p>
<p>This change, however, raises the question: where’s the share button? I’ll let you guess.</p>
<p>Got it? It’s exactly where you expect: hidden behind a long-press gesture on the address bar:</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/12/shortcut-upload-1789202817795-876ed02ed7.png" alt="I still have to watch this Nintendo Direct, by the way."><p class="image-caption">I still have to watch this Nintendo Direct, by the way.</p></div>
<p>As is often the case on modern, post-Liquid Glass iOS, you can generally find what you’re looking for by right-clicking and long-pressing things. Is that great design? I’m not sure. But at least now you know.</p>
</aside><p id="p385">Next up is Apple Intelligence-powered tab organization into topics, which is…<em>passable</em>, but not great. The idea, once again, is sound: wouldn’t it be nice if AI could automatically tidy up your tabs and create topical tab groups for them? This feature works, but its results can be very generic, and can’t be improved or refined with additional prompting or custom instructions (a common refrain in the Apple Intelligence and Siri AI story this year).</p>
<p id="p386">For starters, you have to go hunt for this feature: in Safari’s tab overview, tap the filter button (the three horizontal lines), and you’ll see an option to ‘Organize Tabs’. Once enabled, Safari will start organizing related tabs into topics.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/shortcut-upload-1789123736164-4ddd20adc8.png" alt=""><p class="image-caption"></p></div>
<p id="p388">My issue with this feature is that it can be unreliable and it’s impossible to manage. Sometimes, I’m looking at a collection of tabs that are clearly related, and Safari just doesn’t organize them. There isn’t a button to ping Safari and tell it to organize those tabs, so you’re left <a href="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/14/chatgpt-image-sep-14-2026-10-38-31-am-1789375139957-7144c48560.png" rel="noopener noreferrer">staring at the Apple Intelligence abyss</a> and wondering why it’s not working. Other times, tab organization does work, but the topics are so bland, they might as well not be there. If I’m opening 10 different MacStories tabs, with five about hardware and five about iOS, you’d think that Safari would organize them as “Hardware” and “iOS”, not throw all 10 of them into “MacStories”.</p>
<p id="p389">Since I’ve tested my fair share of AI browsers in the past year, I strongly believe that for AI-based tab grouping to be useful, it needs to be customizable. Let me assign rules or instructions to the browser! Without personalization, and with models that have small context windows and likely can’t process too much tab content at once, we’re left with pretty generic tab topics that aren’t <em>that</em> useful.</p>
<aside class="info-box " id="aside390"><p>Topic-based organization also works for bookmarks. It’s not great there either.</p>
<p class="icon"><svg viewbox="0 0 120 120" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><circle clip-rule="evenodd" cx="60" cy="60" fill-rule="evenodd" r="60"></circle><path d="m44 89v-5h13.2v-28.6h-13.1v-5h19.1v33.6h12.8v5zm10.8-53.1c0-2.8 2.2-4.9 5-4.9s5 2.1 5 4.9-2.2 5-5 5-5-2.1-5-5z" fill="#fff"></path></svg></p>
</aside><p id="p391">The last addition to Safari also marks Apple’s entry into the vibe-coding/“personal software” space for consumers with the ability to create your own Safari extension by describing it. Alas, like the other AI features in Safari, this has been very broken for me during the course of the iOS beta, to the point that – as of the release candidate – I was unable to create any extensions in Safari.</p>
<p id="p392">Again, the theory makes sense: you want to make a fun or productive tweak to Safari? Just describe the extension you want in natural language, and Safari will make it for you. In practice, I can’t comment on this feature since, every time I tried making an extension, Safari would say “Unable to create extension” and recommend other extensions from the App Store instead. I think it’s a nice touch that Apple, despite shipping what is essentially a little vibe-coding product to eschew the App Store altogether, is still suggesting third-party extensions, but we have to wonder how long that’s going to last if the future of computing involves the ability to just create any software you want, the moment you need it.</p>
<p id="p393"><em>Anyway</em>…that’s a topic for another time. For now, I hope Apple can get this feature working.</p>
<h3 id="shortcuts">Shortcuts</h3>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/12/shortcut-upload-1789202943799-7e6d193404.png" alt=""><p class="image-caption"></p></div>
<p id="p395">I decided to close out this chapter with my favorite Apple app of all time, which also happens to be the one that is receiving the most substantial updates in iOS and iPadOS 27: Shortcuts.</p>
<p id="p396">Looking back at the <a href="https://www.macstories.net/stories/ios-and-ipados-26-the-macstories-review/16/#shortcuts" rel="noopener noreferrer">past</a> <a href="https://www.macstories.net/stories/ios-and-ipados-18-the-macstories-review/10/#shortcuts" rel="noopener noreferrer">few</a> <a href="https://www.macstories.net/stories/ios-and-ipados-17-the-macstories-review/12/#shortcuts" rel="noopener noreferrer">years</a> of Shortcuts coverage in my annual iOS reviews, I wouldn’t have guessed we’d end up here again. Year after year, I’ve used this part of the review to complain about the same thing: new actions added to Shortcuts were mostly about toggling settings and launching apps; there was very little in the app for those of us who have been building complex workflows in it since the <a href="https://www.macstories.net/reviews/workflow-review-integrated-automation-for-ios-8/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Workflow days</a>.</p>
<p id="p397">Shortcuts for iOS and iPadOS 27 is different. There are plenty of new actions this year, and a few of them – global variables, reading what’s on screen, Markdown in Notes – fix problems I’ve been working around for over a decade. More importantly, however, Shortcuts is once again paving the way for the future of productivity on Apple platforms, and I can’t even describe how happy it makes me to see Shortcuts thrive and expand as a tool that will democratize automation.</p>
<p id="p398">In a world where any workflow can now be automated with AI and agents, Shortcuts for iOS and iPadOS 27 isn’t bucking the trend: it’s <em>embracing</em> it with new ways to create shortcuts with natural language, actions to tap into Foundation models in the cloud, and deeper integration with Siri AI to easily trigger any of your shortcuts, no matter what you’re doing on your iPhone and iPad. Beyond that, Apple is turning the Shortcuts app into a codified, browsable library of all the capabilities of Siri AI that can be freely remixed by users.</p>
<p id="p399">There’s a lot to get through.</p>
<h4 id="new-actions">New Actions</h4>
<p id="p400">Apple added a variety of actions to the Shortcuts app this year, addressing two features in particular that have been heavily requested in the automation community for years: the ability to store content in Shortcuts with optional global variables, and more flexible conditional blocks.</p>
<h5 id="store-content-get-stored-content-and-delete-stored-content">Store Content, Get Stored Content, and Delete Stored Content</h5>
<p id="p401">Let’s start with the big one.</p>
<p id="p402">Shortcuts <em>finally</em> has a way to save a value and get it back the next time a shortcut runs. There are three new actions for this: ‘Store Content’ saves whatever you pass as input under a name you choose, ‘Get Stored Content’ retrieves it, and ‘Delete Stored Content’ removes it. This system has been elegantly designed so that it’s very intuitive to use, but at the same time flexible enough for power users:</p>
<ul id="ul403"><li>You can store all kinds of text and files with the new Storage actions.</li>
<li>A stored value can be specific to the shortcut it belongs to, or it can be marked as “global”, meaning that all other shortcuts in your library can access it.</li>
<li>At any point, you can open a shortcut’s inspector panel, then select Storage, and review the content stored in the shortcut and available globally. You can change the local or global nature of stored content later, too.</li>
</ul><div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/stored-content-1789142186237-2109bcc3e5.png" alt="Storing content is now possible in Shortcuts."><p class="image-caption">Storing content is now possible in Shortcuts.</p></div>
<p id="p405">If you’ve never needed this, it may sound trivial. Believe me, as someone who has created <em>thousands</em> of shortcuts over the years, it’s not. Until now, if you wanted a shortcut to remember a secure credential, the last item you picked from a list, or whether it had already run today, you had to save a text file in iCloud Drive, rely on a note in the Notes app, or install <a href="https://datajar.app/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Data Jar</a>. I have entire groups of shortcuts in my library that are mostly file-management utilities just to remember a single setting. Over the years, I have created increasingly complex systems to store assets for my <a href="https://www.macstories.net/stories/introducing-apple-frames-4-a-revamped-shortcut-support-for-frame-colors-proportional-scaling-and-the-apple-frames-cli-for-developers/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Apple Frames shortcut</a>, only to eventually give up and use the MacStories CDN. Almost all of that can go away now thanks to the ability to store content inside shortcuts.</p>
<p id="p406">I say “almost” because, alas, it’s not possible to use the Storage actions as a distribution method across multiple users. If I design a new version of Apple Frames to store assets inside itself and share that shortcut with my readers, they won’t be able to retrieve assets stored in my iCloud account inside my personal Shortcuts storage container. This makes sense, and I don’t fault Apple for designing a system that is going to be optimized for individual users, but keep this in mind if you plan on sharing shortcuts that embed content you want other people to use in their own Shortcuts apps.</p>
<aside class="info-box " id="aside407"><p>I’ve asked Apple to clarify the file size limits for stored content in Shortcuts, but I haven’t received a response yet. I’ll update this story when I hear back.</p>
<p class="icon"><svg viewbox="0 0 120 120" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><circle clip-rule="evenodd" cx="60" cy="60" fill-rule="evenodd" r="60"></circle><path d="m44 89v-5h13.2v-28.6h-13.1v-5h19.1v33.6h12.8v5zm10.8-53.1c0-2.8 2.2-4.9 5-4.9s5 2.1 5 4.9-2.2 5-5 5-5-2.1-5-5z" fill="#fff"></path></svg></p>
</aside><p id="p408">I should also stress the importance of the ‘Global Value’ toggle. When you turn it on, the stored content is saved in a special shared space in iCloud, so <em>any</em> shortcut on <em>any</em> of your devices can read from and write to it. This is Apple’s take on global variables, a concept that’s been requested by fellow advanced users of the Shortcuts app on several occasions over the years. With this system, it’s going to be possible to store things like secure credentials, templates, and image assets directly within Shortcuts – no more base64-encoded strings, at last.</p>
<h5 id="get-whats-on-screen-and-get-selected-text">Get What’s On Screen and Get Selected Text</h5>
<p id="p409">As part of on-screen awareness in iOS 27, Apple has completely rebuilt the ‘Get What’s On Screen’ action with support for multiple data types and more flexible configuration options for visible apps.</p>
<p id="p410">With the updated action, you can read one of the following data items visible on screen:</p>
<ul id="ul411"><li>Contacts</li>
<li>Email Addresses</li>
<li>Files</li>
<li>Images</li>
<li>Mail</li>
<li>Messages</li>
<li>Notes</li>
<li>Phone Numbers</li>
<li>Postal Addresses</li>
<li>Reminders</li>
<li>Text</li>
<li>URLs</li>
<li>Voice Memos</li>
<li>Webpages</li>
</ul><p id="p412">Each of these variables does, of course, contain its own set of properties: inspect the variable for on-screen images, for example, and you can see whether it’s a screenshot or screen recording; get a reminder from the screen, and you can see the list it belongs to, or whether it contains images.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/onscreen-variables-1789142192785-10ea978533.png" alt="New properties for on-screen variables."><p class="image-caption">New properties for on-screen variables.</p></div>
<p id="p414">The possibilities of this new action are nearly endless: since it matches Siri’s on-screen awareness, you can do the same things as Siri AI, but programmatically and with controls designed by you. Even better, the action is fully compatible with iPads and the iPhone Duo: a toggle lets you choose whether you want to limit the scope to the visible app only or read from all visible apps.</p>
<p id="p415">There’s also another new action in this category: ‘Get Selected Text’. As the name implies, this action simply gets the text that is currently selected on screen – like the other action, it is best experienced when used via Siri and the Action button, or perhaps as a shortcut from the iPad’s Dock.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/get-text-1789142198272-5656018635.png" alt=""><p class="image-caption"></p></div>
<p id="p417">This action has no parameters. I’ve wanted this for years, mostly because it means text-processing shortcuts no longer have to go through the clipboard or the share sheet. It also means I can now make shortcuts that get Markdown text selected somewhere and publish that as an article on MacStories.</p>
<h5 id="add-item-to-list">Add Item to List</h5>
<p id="p418">Speaking of actions I’ve wanted for years: ‘Add Item to List’ can insert an item into an existing list variable at the beginning, the end, or a specific index.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/add-item-to-list-1789142203351-88445a870f.png" alt=""><p class="image-caption"></p></div>
<p id="p420">If you’ve ever built a Repeat loop just to reconstruct a list with one extra item in the right position, you’ll understand why I’m mentioning such a basic action in the review.</p>
<h5 id="find-vpns-and-get-current-vpn">Find VPNs and Get Current VPN</h5>
<p id="p421"><a href="https://tailscale.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Tailscale</a> and VPN users, this one’s for you.</p>
<p id="p422">The new ‘Find VPNs’ action returns your VPN configurations with different filters as optional parameters; ‘Get Current VPN’ tells you which one is active, if any. Combined with the existing ‘Set VPN’ action, you can now build a proper VPN menu shortcut or an automation that checks whether you’re already connected before doing something else.</p>
<h5 id="messages-2">Messages</h5>
<p id="p423">The Messages app received a variety of actions this year that confirm how Apple is thinking about Shortcuts now: it needs to expose the same actions that are otherwise available in Siri AI. The new actions are:</p>
<ul id="ul424"><li>Delete Conversations</li>
<li>Delete Messages</li>
<li>Mark as Read</li>
<li>Open Inbox</li>
<li>Search in Messages</li>
<li>Send Tapback</li>
</ul><div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/shortcut-upload-1789168325083-6fbfb198e1.png" alt=""><p class="image-caption"></p></div>
<p id="p426">As you can see, these actions essentially reconstruct what you can do with Messages and Siri AI, but inside Shortcuts, with support for variables and user-defined flows. I especially like the ‘Send Tapback’ action, which reacts to a specific message with one of the six standard Tapbacks (Love, Like, Dislike, Laugh, Emphasize, and Question). To use this action effectively, you’ll have to pair it with the ‘Find Messages’ action, which needs to retrieve a variable for the specific message from the Messages app first.</p>
<h5 id="photos-2">Photos</h5>
<p id="p427">As with Messages, Photos gained a bunch of new actions that match the capabilities of Siri AI:</p>
<ul id="ul428"><li>Auto Enhance Photo</li>
<li>Delete Albums</li>
<li>Favorite Photos</li>
<li>Find Albums</li>
<li>Hide Photos</li>
<li>Open Photo</li>
</ul><p id="p429">While I’ve never played around with Photos automation much, I’m very intrigued by the idea of scripting my albums in iOS 27.</p>
<h5 id="reminders-2">Reminders</h5>
<p id="p430">I’m very happy to see Reminders – which has been my task manager for almost a year at this point, a new record for me – receive some great new actions in iOS 27:</p>
<ul id="ul431"><li>Create Group</li>
<li>Create Section</li>
<li>Delete Groups</li>
<li>Delete Lists</li>
<li>Delete Sections</li>
<li>Edit List</li>
</ul><p id="p432">Sadly, the ‘New Reminder’ action still does not support adding a task to a specific section of a list – a strange omission given improved section support in other actions this year.</p>
<h4 id="updates-to-existing-actions">Updates to Existing Actions</h4>
<p id="p433">In addition to new actions, I want to mention some updates to existing actions:</p>
<h5 id="notes-markdown-at-last">Notes: Markdown, at Last</h5>
<p id="p434">As I mentioned before, Notes’ ‘Create Note’ and ‘Append to Note’ actions now support Markdown as text input. The ‘Append to Note’ action has also gained a ‘Section’ parameter to choose where in the note the text should go and an ‘Ignore Empty Lines’ option.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/shortcut-upload-1789167580667-0a06cff0f5.png" alt=""><p class="image-caption"></p></div>
<p id="p436">If you’ve built a journaling or logging shortcut around Notes – and I know a lot of you have – this replaces an entire category of workarounds.</p>
<h5 id="if-otherwise-if">If: Otherwise If</h5>
<p id="p437">Eight years after Apple relaunched Workflow as Shortcuts, it has added support for “else if” branches inside conditional blocks.</p>
<p id="p438">Two years ago, I wrote about compound conditions making If blocks more readable; this is the other half that was missing from that redesign of conditionals. Programmers should obviously be familiar with the concept of “else if”, but if you’ve ever played around with advanced shortcuts, I’m sure you know what I’m talking about here. Instead of nesting a separate ‘If’ block inside an ‘Otherwise’ inside <em>another</em> ‘If’, you chain the conditions in a single block now. That’s really all there is to it, and this more elegant approach has also resulted in <em>dramatically</em> better performance for multi-condition flows in the Shortcuts editor.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/otherwise-if-1789142210904-87eff183ab.png" alt=""><p class="image-caption"></p></div>
<p id="p440">My older shortcuts are full of indented conditionals that I’m now going to rebuild.</p>
<h5 id="use-model-follow-up-cloud-pro-and-world-knowledge">Use Model: Follow Up, Cloud Pro, and World Knowledge</h5>
<p id="p441">The ‘Use Model’ action has gained a couple of new options, matching what is also possible in Siri AI. First, Cloud Pro is available as a model option for tasks that require deeper reasoning, although it does <em>not</em> let users set a specific reasoning level like third-party developers can in their apps.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/cloud-pro-and-web-1789142219111-af6d279823.png" alt=""><p class="image-caption"></p></div>
<p id="p443">Additionally, there’s a new ‘Use Broad World Knowledge’ toggle that lets the model answer by tapping into Apple’s proprietary world knowledge database. In my tests, flipping this switch and combining it with Cloud Pro for web searches has produced some pretty good, up-to-date results.</p>
<h5 id="maps-avoid-highways-and-avoid-tolls">Maps: Avoid Highways and Avoid Tolls</h5>
<p id="p444">The ‘Get Travel Time’ action now has ‘Avoid Highways’ and ‘Avoid Tolls’ toggles, so your “time to leave” shortcut can finally match how you drive.</p>
<h5 id="extract-text-from-image-text-lists-and-tables">Extract Text from Image: Text, Lists, and Tables</h5>
<p id="p445">‘Extract Text from Image’ has a new parameter with three options: Text, Lists, and Tables.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/shortcut-upload-1789167641156-be96ddee96.png" alt=""><p class="image-caption"></p></div>
<p id="p447">The last two mean you can point the action at a photo of a list or a table and get structured output instead of a flat block of text. This matches the vision capabilities of Siri AI in Visual Intelligence, and I’m especially keen to combine these with adding structured content to Apple Notes.</p>
<h5 id="scan-qr-or-barcode-extract-mode">Scan QR or Barcode: Extract Mode</h5>
<p id="p448">‘Scan QR or Barcode’ has two modes now: Scan opens the camera like before; Extract reads a code from an image you pass in as input.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/shortcut-upload-1789167704506-86e0126751.png" alt=""><p class="image-caption"></p></div>
<p id="p450">Yes, this means you no longer have to point your iPhone at your own screen to read a QR code in a screenshot. I’ve done that more times than I’d like to admit.</p>
<h5 id="safari-create-tab-group">Safari: Create Tab Group</h5>
<p id="p451">The ‘Create Tab Group’ action got a ‘Contents’ parameter that accepts URLs or existing tabs, so you can create and populate a tab group with a single action.</p>
<h5 id="new-reminder-urgent">New Reminder: Urgent</h5>
<p id="p452">While it didn’t receive support for sections, ‘New Reminder’ did get an ‘Urgent’ toggle, matching the modern urgency feature from the Reminders app last year.</p>
<h5 id="get-network-details-ethernet">Get Network Details: Ethernet</h5>
<p id="p453">‘Get Network Details’ received an Ethernet option alongside Wi-Fi and cellular, with nine fields:</p>
<ul id="ul454"><li>Interface Name</li>
<li>IPv4 Address</li>
<li>IPv6 Address</li>
<li>Subnet Mask</li>
<li>Router IP</li>
<li>Is Connected</li>
<li>Link Speed</li>
<li>Ethernet Standard</li>
<li>Hardware MAC Address</li>
</ul><h5 id="set-volume">Set Volume</h5>
<p id="p455">Matching what is now possible in the Clock app, the ‘Set Volume’ action now lets you automate volume levels for alarms and timers, or alerts and system sounds, separately from media and ringtone.</p>
