<h4 id="automations">Automations</h4>
<p id="p470">Alongside the plethora of new actions we’re getting in Shortcuts this year, my other favorite change is the complete redesign of automations: they’re no longer a separate section of the app, but instead appear as “special actions” at the very top of a shortcut, which means that you can also share shortcuts that contain automations with other users. <em>Finally</em>.</p>
<p id="p471">You can add an automation to a shortcut either via natural language (more on this in the next section), or by opening the action library in the editor and selecting the new ‘Automation’ category. Doing this will let you browse the catalog of automation triggers, and you can tap one to add it to the shortcut, where it’ll always be placed at the top, ready to be configured.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/show-inline-automation-at-top-of-shortcut-1789147901399-9335e90f23.png" alt="Automations are now actions at the top of a shortcut."><p class="image-caption">Automations are now actions at the top of a shortcut.</p></div>
<p id="p473">Because automation triggers are actions, though, it also means something else: we can now stack multiple automations in the same shortcut! Power users will love this: it lets us run the same shortcut when any of several conditions is met, instead of creating a separate trigger for each one, as was the case in iOS 26.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/multiple-triggers-1789142230117-17fd7ba233.png" alt="Multiple triggers for the same shortcut."><p class="image-caption">Multiple triggers for the same shortcut.</p></div>
<p id="p475">Apple has designed the sharing flow thoughtfully: to avoid the risk of someone else installing a shortcut from the web that contains an automation they don’t know about, automations will be disabled by default (and visibly grayed out) on the receiver’s end upon installation. To enable an automation, you’ll have to flip its main toggle at the top of a shortcut, and then it’ll be able to run with configuration options previously set by the creator of the shortcut.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/keyboard-automation-turned-off-and-on-1789147897466-75ba161b79.png" alt="A disabled automation (left)."><p class="image-caption">A disabled automation (left).</p></div>
<p id="p477">You can still browse all shortcuts that contain automations in a dedicated Automations page of the app, which also lets you quickly turn them on or off, filter them, and see the ones that you haven’t finished setting up.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/show-ios-27-automations-page-two-phones-side-by-side-with-lots-of-automations-1789147906129-a70ed2aebb.png" alt=""><p class="image-caption"></p></div>
<p id="p479">As a longtime Shortcuts creator, I’m incredibly happy to see automations become something I can share directly with my readers instead of being a fiddly feature I need to explain how to set up manually. This is an excellent change, and having automations live in the editor also makes them easier to configure and tweak.</p>
<p id="p480">In addition to unified automations, there are also new automation types to choose from. With the new <strong>Keyboard</strong> trigger, you can configure a shortcut to run when any keyboard is connected to or disconnected from your device. This could be particularly handy with an iPad, for instance, if you want to switch to specific Home Screens or Focus modes when the Magic Keyboard is detached.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/keyboard-automation-1789142247376-b979b1779d.png" alt=""><p class="image-caption"></p></div>
<p id="p482">There’s also a <strong>Notification</strong> trigger. With this automation, you can let a shortcut run when you receive a notification from a specific app and optionally filter the title, subtitle, or message contained in the notification itself. You can even process a dedicated ‘Notification’ magic variable in the editor for further checks against its body text, time-sensitive nature, or date.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/notification-1789142253496-6102ad2ee5.png" alt=""><p class="image-caption"></p></div>
<p id="p484">While I’m the type of person who tries to reduce incoming notifications as much as possible, I’ll admit that I see lots of potential for fun use cases of this automation trigger, such as flashing your HomeKit lights red when a food delivery is nearby, unlocking the front door when your spouse’s car pulls into the garage, and more.</p>
<h4 id="describe-a-shortcut">Describe a Shortcut</h4>
<p id="p485">I saved the most important feature of the Shortcuts app this year for last: you can now describe shortcuts with natural language, and AI will make them for you, in seconds, right inside Shortcuts.</p>
<p id="p486">I knew that this was going to happen soon when I released my <a href="https://github.com/viticci/shortcuts-playground-plugin" rel="noopener noreferrer">Shortcuts Playground plugin for Codex and Claude</a> earlier this year, and you may think that I’m upset that Apple “sherlocked” me with this feature. So, let me state this very clearly:</p>
<p id="p487">I’m ecstatic that Apple is shipping this feature in Shortcuts for free, and that they’ve made it so easy to use. The biggest barrier to entry for the Shortcuts app has always been teaching people how to use the editor and connect actions together: with the ability to describe a shortcut and will it into existence in a few seconds, that barrier has been destroyed. Apple has done an excellent job making it fast and reliable at generating simple- to medium-complexity shortcuts.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/shortcut-upload-1789167917861-95761132b1.png" alt="Generating a shortcut with an automation in iOS 27."><p class="image-caption">Generating a shortcut with an automation in iOS 27.</p></div>
<p id="p489">See, that’s why I’m not upset: Apple’s natural language shortcut creation cannot generate highly complex shortcuts that span hundreds of actions, or go off to the web to research an API and then make a shortcut for it. My Shortcuts Playground plugin can literally make and remix Apple Frames (I made version 4.0 of the shortcut with it!); Apple’s feature will stop short of generating anything that involves reasoning for longer than 30 seconds. There is still plenty of space in this field for an agent-agnostic, long-running plugin such as Shortcuts Playground to exist. (In fact, I’ll have a major announcement about it soon.)</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/shortcut-upload-1789168227586-c760dc7391.png" alt=""><p class="image-caption"></p></div>
<aside class="pull-quote aligncenter" id="aside491"><blockquote>
<p>Describe a Shortcut is a marvel of engineering and, possibly, the most impressive demo of Foundation models to date.</p>
</blockquote>
</aside><p id="p492">And here’s another thing: a simpler, faster, more reliable version of Shortcuts Playground made by Apple is exactly what I wanted and needed as a user. The ‘Describe a Shortcut’ feature can one-shot all kinds of shortcuts in 15 seconds or so. It can pre-fill all supported parameters in all supported actions. It can pre-scan parameters during the generation phase to make sure they’re valid (such as checking whether a note exists if you ask to make a shortcut to append to a specific note). It can configure all supported toggles in each action for you. It’s also beautifully designed with a gorgeous Liquid Glass animation, and it supports editing shortcuts with natural language by describing further changes to them.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper video-autoplay js-video-autoplay aligncenter aligncenter aligncenter aligncenter aligncenter aligncenter" id="div493">
            <video data-autoplay="yes" width="1500" height="1150" data-is-retina="yes" preload="auto" playsinline controls><source src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/12/generate-shortcut-framed-1789217222624-709142be22.mp4" type="video/mp4"><source src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/12/generate-shortcut-framed-1789217222624-709142be22.webm" type="video/webm"><source src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/12/generate-shortcut-framed-1789217222624-709142be22.ogv" type="video/ogg"></source></source></source></video><div class="caption">
<p>Generating a shortcut in iPadOS 27.</p>
</div></div>
<p id="p494">In case it is not clear enough: Describe a Shortcut is a marvel of engineering and, possibly, the most impressive demo of Foundation models to date. It’s no surprise that Apple believes in this feature so strongly that it’s now the default screen when you create a new shortcut in the app.</p>
<aside class="info-box " id="aside495"><p>You can still make the Shortcuts app open the classic editor by default in Settings. When ‘Describe a Shortcut’ is the default, the iPad layout of the app also changes to a sweet three-pane configuration in landscape.</p>
<p class="icon"><svg viewbox="0 0 120 120" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><circle clip-rule="evenodd" cx="60" cy="60" fill-rule="evenodd" r="60"></circle><path d="m44 89v-5h13.2v-28.6h-13.1v-5h19.1v33.6h12.8v5zm10.8-53.1c0-2.8 2.2-4.9 5-4.9s5 2.1 5 4.9-2.2 5-5 5-5-2.1-5-5z" fill="#fff"></path></svg></p>
</aside><p id="p496">I’ve been testing this feature all summer, and while it’s not capable of making shortcuts for third-party apps (I assume this will be coming in the future) or third-party web APIs not in its knowledge base, it is an excellent starting point for most users of the Shortcuts app. I once called Shortcuts <a href="https://www.macstories.net/stories/ios-12-the-macstories-review/16/#conclusion" rel="noopener noreferrer">a bicycle for the mind</a>; the ability to turn an idea into a working automation is the ultimate expression of that philosophy. This is what computers should do: they should help us spend less time managing the computer itself, and more time doing what we actually like.</p>
<p id="p497">Go play around with ‘Describe a Shortcut’, test its limits, and have fun with it. I bet it’s going to teach you some things you didn’t know about Shortcuts, and, most importantly, it’ll help you do things with your iPhone and iPad you didn’t think were possible.</p>
<p id="p498"><!--nextpage--></p>
<div class="cover-image cover-image-themed" id="div499">
    <img decoding="async" class="cover-image-content cover-image-dark" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/14/shortcut-upload-1789384132672-f33c9dd11d.png" alt="" loading="lazy" data-low-fi-src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/14/shortcut-upload-1789384137056-0a54566222.png"><img decoding="async" class="cover-image-content cover-image-light" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/14/shortcut-upload-1789384099791-16a0930635.png" alt="" loading="lazy" data-low-fi-src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/14/shortcut-upload-1789384103960-bebd3bf361.png"></div>
<h2 id="everything-else">Everything Else</h2>
<p id="p500">As is always the case with new versions of iOS and iPadOS, changes in the operating systems run the gamut from tentpole features to quality-of-life improvements and smaller tweaks that you may not notice at first.</p>
<p id="p501">Let’s take a look at everything else that’s new in iOS and iPadOS 27.</p>
<h3 id="airpods">AirPods</h3>
<p id="p502">In iOS 27, Apple completely revamped the AirPods management page in Settings, going from a bland, long list of toggles and sub-menus to a more colorful page that neatly organizes everything into richer menus:</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/10/shortcut-upload-1789031425035-de95d155c8.png" alt=""><p class="image-caption"></p></div>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/10/shortcut-upload-1789031445811-78bc9b65f3.png" alt=""><p class="image-caption"></p></div>
<p id="p505">As you can see, a volume slider was added underneath the different listening modes supported by compatible AirPods, but that’s not all. When selecting ‘Adaptive’ mode in the top slider, iOS 27 will reveal new options to make its intensity lighter (leaning more toward Transparency mode) or stronger (toward Noise Cancellation). I’m glad Apple did this: I never fully understood Adaptive mode, and I’ve long preferred to toggle between Transparency and Noise Cancellation because I felt like Adaptive was suppressing too much background noise for my taste. I’ve set the new slider between Transparency and Adaptive and, so far, it’s working well.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/14/shortcut-upload-1789388595206-d5b5511890.png" alt=""><p class="image-caption"></p></div>
<p id="p507">I’ve never been into equalizers myself, but iOS 27 also introduces <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/how-to/set-custom-eq-airpods-ios/" rel="noopener noreferrer">custom EQ support</a> for AirPods Pro 3, AirPods 4 and later, and AirPods Max 2. Unfortunately, I haven’t been able to test this since the option to install AirPods beta firmware never appeared on my devices.</p>
<h3 id="cellular">Cellular</h3>
<p id="p508">As part of iOS 27’s focus on performance improvements across the board, Apple brought a couple of useful changes to how the system handles cellular and Wi-Fi connections behind the scenes.</p>
<p id="p509">First, iOS 27 intelligently transitions between Wi-Fi and cellular as you move around, keeping your data connection working without you having to go into Control Center to toggle Wi-Fi off.</p>
<p id="p510">I know firsthand that this works because I used to run into this very problem every single day. You see, in the 10 meters that separate our apartment’s front door from the elevator on our floor, my iPhone would stay connected to Wi-Fi in the apartment, but I couldn’t actually do anything because data wouldn’t work. Like every flawed human being, I must take advantage of the 30 seconds that I spend waiting for the elevator to open social media and doomscroll. iOS 27 fixes this: as soon as I walk out of the house, the system detects a weak connection, intelligently re-routes it to cellular, and I can get to my timeline while I mindlessly wait for the elevator to arrive. Maybe I shouldn’t have wished for Apple to fix this? In any case, the feature works well – and it’s one of those things that you’ll stop noticing and take for granted until you go back and experience what iOS 26 used to be.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/10/new-design-for-lack-of-wifi-connection-1789031377297-7883338ec2.png" alt=""><p class="image-caption"></p></div>
<p id="p512">Second, there’s a new design for notifications that tell you about Wi-Fi connections that aren’t working. I hope you never get this.</p>
<h3 id="keyboard">Keyboard</h3>
<p id="p513">There are always keyboard-related updates in this chapter of the review, and iOS 27 does not break tradition. I mentioned this before in the Siri AI chapter, but it bears repeating here: the larger, more powerful AFM 3 Core Advanced on-device model (which is only supported on modern devices) enables a faster, more reliable dictation experience.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/10/shortcut-upload-1789031144256-fca0ca627d.png" alt=""><p class="image-caption"></p></div>
<p id="p515">You probably won’t notice the differences at first since the UI looks the same, but in my experience, the new dictation is way more accurate than the previous one. As someone who uses an iPhone in English with an Italian accent, I’ve noticed that iOS 27 transcribes certain words more accurately than iOS 26, such as “wide”, which iOS 26 heard as “why” in the comparison above.</p>
<p id="p516">As you start using iOS 27, you’ll also notice two more additions to the keyboard experience. First, there’s system-wide automatic proofreading that checks whatever you’ve typed and underlines suggestions in blue. This is not autocorrect: proofreading checks for correctly typed words that do not fit in the context of a sentence – such as “this is a tests”, where the word “tests” would be underlined. You can accept suggestions by tapping on them.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/10/shortcut-upload-1789031887763-43356f4bda.png" alt="Automatic proofreading."><p class="image-caption">Automatic proofreading.</p></div>
<p id="p518">Second, when you copy something to the clipboard – whether it’s some text, a link, or an image – the next time you select a text field, you’ll see a suggestion above the keyboard to instantly paste what you just copied.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/11/shortcut-upload-1789171153499-ad400c200d.png" alt="Another banger from Apple’s quality-of-life department."><p class="image-caption">Another banger from Apple’s quality-of-life department.</p></div>
<p id="p520">It’s a small tweak, but it’s one of those time-saving features that Apple drops out of nowhere every year and that become instant classics. I think millions of people will love this enhancement to the keyboard.</p>
<h3 id="and-more">And More…</h3>
<p id="p521">More features? <em>More features.</em> Let’s bring this home:</p>
<p id="p522"><strong>There is a new Mac-like recovery mode.</strong> As <a href="https://9to5mac.com/2026/06/11/iphone-ipad-ios-27-recovery-boot-mode/" rel="noopener noreferrer">reported by 9to5Mac</a>, there’s a brand new recovery flow in iOS 27 that resembles the page you get on macOS when you need to recover your device. If you see this screen, something’s gone wrong.</p>
<p id="p523"><strong>A new design for Apple Pay on the web.</strong> The Apple Pay sheet has a new look when you’re buying something on the web. I like how it handles multiple associated cards because it makes it clearer how to switch between them.</p>
<p id="p524"><strong>Notifications go sideways.</strong> For reasons that, in hindsight, make more sense because of the iPhone Duo, notification banners now appear with a new animation that swoops in from the top-left corner of the screen. This is not just cosmetic or optimized for the Duo, though: the animation also serves to reinforce that you can swipe from the top-left corner to open Notification Center, as opposed to swiping from the center, which opens Siri AI now.</p>
<p id="p525"><strong>Gotta go fast.</strong> Most reviews of iOS and iPadOS 27 (is anyone else still writing them?) will probably only focus on this part of the experience, and highlight it at the beginning of their narrative. I saved it for the end, because I think it’s important to see what iOS 27 does differently before appreciating how much faster it makes everything.</p>
<p id="p526">There’s no way around it: iOS 27 is the most reliable, fastest, smoothest version of iOS Apple has ever released. From smoother unlocks and scrolling Home Screen pages to better performance under heavy load (thanks to a new CPU scheduler, a part of the OS Apple <a href="https://www.macstories.net/stories/interview-craig-federighi-opens-up-about-ipados-its-multitasking-journey-and-the-ipads-essence/" rel="noopener noreferrer">has been improving a lot lately</a>) and 30% faster app launches (courtesy of preloading app data), there is virtually no part of iOS 27 that hasn’t been affected by Apple’s focus on performance this year.</p>
<p id="p527">You have to see it to believe it. Which is why I’d recommend you go to Settings, load up Software Update, and upgrade from iOS 26 as quickly as you can.</p>
